      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-04-26_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>DZAR NELLIE,</p>
        <p>Buttercups and cowslips look very well with loops and bows of black velvet or panne on a white straw shape. It has always been a matter of wonder that the primrose is so little employed in millinery, but I have just seen a toque made entirely of the " rath" flower, and charming it looked, market bunches of pale primroses and their leaves, tied together with narrow black velvet ribbon, and set close together over the invisible foundation. The toque was small, as are all the newest and best toques, tilted up on the left side, and bent over the Lice on the right. This is the correct poise of the toque of the day, and proves a becoming one on the whole. They are easier to wear than the very large hats now the fashion; these require t:. • most careful of coiffures, and need a good deal of dressing up to. A most per- fect example of the grand chapeau was a soft rich straw, the beefeater sort of crown tied in with a broad band of black velvet, the left side turned up very high and crumpled, and underneath one of the widest, fullest, and longest black ostrich feathers possible to image, sweeping on to the hair behind, the stem in hoot hidden by a bow of old. lace, clasped by a diamond broock.</p>
        <p>borne of the new boleros have a deep collar turned back from the top and hanging over the shoulders and the top of the arms. One of these is wade of black tucked chiffon. The collar is in tucked chiffon with a border of white insertion, and a slightly fulled edging of the lace all round it. Another is in jetted net, the collar matching it, but bordered all round with an inch-wide bias band of grey and white panne. This collar forms two points on each shoulder. The square collars in white net appliqué, with rich designs in cream or biscuit.coloured lace, are very telling. Black accordeon-pleated chiffon is used by the acre every day in making up blouses for day and evening wear. One wonders what the smart world did without this most useful of materials, and alsc without the pleating which won so much favour from its first introduction some 15 years since. and has maintained it ever since.</p>
        <p>Mosel is to play a large part in this season trimmings, both for day and evening wear. &amp;alas of the new belts have flowers with raised petals in soft, coloured silks, sewn round with a chain stitch in gold or silver tinsel on a back-ground of gold or silver. Gold is preferred, as a rule, exeept when a half-mourning tint is imperative. With the bodices that are cut away round the throat, the neck is sometimes finished with this floral and tinsel embroidery, coming down in a point in front, and growing shallow towards the shoulders. The sleeves are usually finished in the same 15113, .</p>
        <p>Apxopos of hike ties, imitations are now se oximellent that everybody wears them and the hal- - lotion Irish point can scarcely be distinguished ; from the original. Of course, real lace is a wood illMsestment when it is the genuine thing, foal not get ap merely for tempting the unwary in theqi "ilea; etc. There is a great satinfaCtion in the ion of good real lace; but it is a satisfaction-- Ty":: "means without alloy. One hates to eat it, and yet it is hardly ever the right shape or length for the purpose to which one wishes to apply it. And then it soils just as easily as the imitation, and one cireada to trust it to th 3 tender mercies of the ordinary cleaner. Altogether real lace is often a white elephant to the possessor, Who has nut also the ingenuity and patience to twist and turn it to account, and the industry to clean and mend it herself. A pretty bedspread can be made of tlaimed netting, which is mice more coming into fashion again. The net may be bought or hanil-made, hut in any case it must be in squares measuring from 6 to Bin. each way, and a similar number (the exact quantity of squares required of course depends on the size of the bed) of pieces of white linen of the same siie must be prepared. Each net square is to be darned with a suitaole pattern with white em- treoidery cotton, and each linen one adorned with a conventional but graceful spray worked in satin- eti4ch with turquoise-blue ingrain cotton. When the rat/slake number of squares have been com- pleted, they are to be very neatly joined together alternately, and all the seams worked over with feather-stitching done in blue. The bedspread should Cattily be lined with turquoise blue sateen and trimmed either with a frill of the same or with crochet or netted lace. The spread may, of course, be worked entirely in white, the colour being introduced in the lining only, or, on the other hand, the darning on the net may be executed in coloured cotton as well as the embroidery on the linen. An all-white spread looks particularly nice lined with rose-pink or apple-green. Your loving J9,7)rry _</p>
        <p>31 ODO UG L'S PATH= SELF-RAISING ELOU It ALROP"</p>
