      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-04-26_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>E p psgs GriATEFUL-COMFORTING. COCOA 3REAKFAST-PU PREP.</head>
        <p>E p psgs GriATEFUL-COMFORTING. COCOA 3REAKFAST-PU PREP.</p>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>The Duke and Ducbees of Fife are stay/in°, at Blythe- wood for the opening of the Glasgow Exhibe ien, which is to take place on Net 2nd. Queen Vreoria aoaYed at Blythswood for sty-tat days in 1888, and the truse which is bcth large and modern, is well arranged. The sronnds comprise eoire fine woods, and beautiful gar- dens. from which there is a good view of the Clyde. The Duke aria Duchess will make a stay of eeveral days, after which they sill return to Seeen Lodge. Richmond, where they will reside with their children, until leaving for I), rails/ in July. Of all the beautiful to- me' over which the Duchese of Ff. presides. Sheen L-dge—little more than a cottage 'huh it is—remains her favourite. Here, anything in the way of formal entertaining is out of the question,- a re'ief for which the Duche.s is undoubt, city very grate- ful • but airimly family gathersege are much indulged in during the time Her Royal Highness is in residence. The grounds, which are exceedingly beautiful, contain a little tea house, in the form of tt Chinese yagodayarel here it is, that the Duchess, on sunny afierneons, informally dispenses tea, the merry borne-party she loves to gather round her Tne Bev n. s Burdett-Coutte, who has just entered upon her eight 3 -eighth year, is certainly the "Grand Old Lady" of the present day. Born before, Queen. Victoria, she came lumber fortune of nearly £2,000,000 18.37, the year Per late Queen began to roign. At that time, such a sum Wad Considered a great fortune, theugh Pew,when we occalliellaily Rear of multi-mllien- airee, giving away millions at a time,- it Weted not be thought anything out of the way. Few 1261rfer'Jr7 if any, have put their wealth ,tef bettet mos than 118 Baroness Burdett Coot!". For over Silty ;IPS Ilk has regarded her fortune as Wend Of tut for the carrying am of I good' weenie !P1 derive thef t-trytf.he melt have lateen • - kicky in imetinie aom/or the public (00 , ' It is a common remark that the entry of women into all kinds et business occupations has made it more d Ili- cult for men to fled the means So marry. This is no doubt p3rtiall icorrsetehougb th- rquens recently issued by the Registr a. General, co not bear, out. this theca y. It is true ti at in many employments, such as shorthand, typewriting, warehouse, and office we k generally, the comretition of women must lead to haver salaries, but society as a whole, must be letter of!. no that the great bulk of women " dos mething "instead of tett( r- ing away their time at home, as under the old condi- tions. If it meave that some men have to accept fewer salaries, it also means that tie y bays not to ccntribute to the support of their sisters, and other womenlolklof their families, and they thus obtain a freedom from ter- ponsibility, which they cLa not-formerly( enjoy. There is a new craze, which promises to become:quite fashionable, viz, the manufacture 'of hertat-tnade sweets. It has peen started by e ladeobetorgiog to a wer-to-do lam ly, who had ere:lobed some art in confec- tionery making. 'Me kw wleoge sbe turn, d tlaccouet by producing all Kett; of niceties, al hich she sold amongst her friends.who,is theit bun, took up the idea, with the result that it is rapidly becoming a lashionable hobby. One lady has opened,* high class shop.but that could scarcely be done with only home-made sweets,see- ing that expensive machieery is. riqiiiiedfor the macu • facture of many varieties of the cern kinds of confection- ery. Still there is no more reason why women should not start fashionable sweetetuff theps than millinery seed other businem establishments, of which there are many in the West End of London, owned and mattered by titled wcmen and by those belonging to the land- owning families. The motive is probably net so much that of mere money-makirg as the desire for an indepeodent livelihood, which is now characteristic of all classes, ra the world of women. Ri centle quite a number of women have been made churchwardens, and the question having been raised as to the legality of such appointments, the Law Journal t. k's upon itself to declare that they are quite in order. Not Only may vicars make their own wives churchwar- dens, but they may also be over-eers, and even sextons. These matters are carried atilt further in emerica,where women parsers have rIB late) for years. In that .eountry too they sometimes appoint the, best looking, aad the beat dressed girls to act, /14 "sidesmen," and to hand round the bag or plate. That is not because, they I perform these offices belt. r than theemen, but because it ,has been found that those .churches with such attrac- tions fill better than the old-fashioned ones. Over here many vicara declare, that women make better ceurcti- wardens than thetsen, snd that is quite easy to under. nd ID the nutter of Feerr`Law :Administration, It is generaPy conceded that the lady guardians are the mast • IM,ient, and this seems to he borne out by the steadily increasing number of wom?n now returned upon the Bnards, throughout the country. At the recent conversazione of the Japan boriety, at Princes Hall (London) none of the charming Japarese women who live among us, appeared in their native costume, a fact with% was much regretted. To lovers of the pictureique, the kimonos is a truly delightful iform of attire. Straight from tin- neck to the feet i• falls, and folds over the front, being s' curd at the waist with a bro.d sash,known as 'he oii, .hich formawait band, and is arranged behind in a hire knot or bow: At the nes kit is worn open, disclosing a tied of some different shade. --</p>
        <p>The colours mostly affected by Joanese women are ' dull greys or blues, or exquisite shadet of dove,or fswn Their sashes are of some light colour, usually lined with SCailet, and the tame colour as the smsh is made of repeated in the lining of the loose wise sleeves, which are partly awn up in front to form a pocket. Women of rank have their kimoncs built of silk, richly orea- mented with gold cr silver filwerp,which are woven into the nisi, riel. A sweet ard clairty vision is the Jspsiet,te lady in esti ye costume, and those who were looking tor-</p>
        <p>ward to many such at the Princes Hall Conversaziooe, met with a wry keen di,appoif.tment. Leading milliners are offering neadgear aid flies to match, an excellf nt ides, as this ia often a very difficult Matter when making such purchases at different times and at different establishmetes Regarding headgear, the straws of the moment are wondertully soft, both in texture and truch, the rhiuy variety b-iee telers te only in the herd, purely utilitarian sailor. Tcrouehout the rarly ipring we were fold repeatedly that tne day of the sailor bat was past ai d gone.sci far as the fashion- able world is concerned. Lire the navy 'serge, and the blouse however, Ihe sailor hat is still with 119, and promises to remain. Very lowly are the new map ins, now oeing offered us. With the excellent wearing, qualitii s possessed by our old friend we are, me.t of us, well acquainted, and the fabric not only appears as strong and durable as ever, but has gained considerably in beauty sinc• it was last in evidence. Much of it, appearing under the name Of c èpeline, haa a silk surface, and - this soft delicate fabric is eminently suit( d for the draped and tucked skirts which will be all the vogue for some months to come. Men fires are extinguished for the season, some de- vice tadesfrable for concealing the emir', hearth. An excellent r•cre.n is provided ov a flat•baokd bamboo stand, wit,: matting shelves, projecting bete and there into the room. Behind the shelves, framed as it were by the bamboo surround„-,shoul4 be stretched a length of tapektry: No large piece is nrces,ary, so it should han-l.oine in quality, and gorgeous incehuring. yet Withal hai monising with the prevailing tints of the ' room, And especially with the china, and other orna- ments stood upon the shelves. Ruch a stand as ale, pushed awl( back upon the hearth, conceals all that is PClesireble, yet admit thi neeeary sir all round it. 1 " MAPOE.'</p>
        <p>Pure Blood. Your heart beats over One hundred thousand times each day. One hundred thousand supplies, of good or bad blood to your brains Which is, it? If bad, impure blood, then your head aches. You are troubled with drowsiness, yet cannot sleep; you are as tired in the morning as at night; you have no nerve power; your fOOO does not seem to do you much good. Purgative, tonics,. headache ,n.owden,4 cannot- pure you. What is require:V.% something ,to 'make the kidneys perform their proper work, when they will remove all impurities from the blond and (. make it rich in it life-giving properties. The kidneys, if weak or overworked, cannot do this unaided—to give them the aid required take</p>
      </div>
