        <p>A ivarringtrs!v Painter's Sad End. Mr. S. Brighous-, ()minty Coroner, conducted an inquest at the Polire Sration'on Monday evening ' touching the death of Harry Johnson (22),a painter . residing at 9. Kendrick-street, whose body wasl ' recovered from the Mersey at Bishop's Wharf on I Sunday morning Martha Harriet Potter, wife of Isaac Potter. a file cutter, living at 17, Albert- ten ace, &amp;why, stated that the deceased was her son by her first hu•band. She last saw him alive at three o'eock on Faturday afternoon when he appeared to to in his usual good health and spirits lie told her that 1e womd call it her house for a basket which he had left there in the j evening. She 'never saw him again until she identified his body on Sunday. If be had been coming to the hou,e, he would have passed the spot where his body was 1000d.7-Edith Rawlinson, wife of Albert Riwlinson, a clerk residing at 9, Kendlick-street,said the deceased had lodged with her for the past month, during which time he had been very intemperate. About ;11.15 !on Saturday night became in the house drunk. Hui supper was r+ ady for him and owing to his condi- tion, witness did not at first speak to him. She: tl v afterwards he paid" it was Lime he was baVil4l something to eat "so she then spoke to him about being out so late. fie then got up and left,,the house and she never saw him again.—P S. Evans said that at 81. on Sundßy morning he was et Bishops Wan f nd saw the body f the deceased in ih. river. ne. Onee obtained ass'stanc,. awl got the body out of the water. He removed it ¶0 the "Seven Stars' Inn. and examined it hut found n 1 marks of violence.—A verdict of "found drc)wned- was r. - turned,</p>
