      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-04-26_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <p>THE EX3:%IINETZ, FR`l) Ir, APRIL 2ft, 1901</p>
        <p>( Coi:yng4t.)</p>
        <p>I "Clan&amp; of England ? " ig yee.”</p>
        <p>'• What rind or an eaucation oia you receive r "I passed through Harrow and Oxford," said Adari;n "Oh I" said the schoolmaster, and without further, comment pissed out, and Martin never set eyes on him again. Later in the day, after he had vainly endeavoured to swallow the breakfast of coarse skilly which was brought, another visitor was ushered in. His calling was evident at a glance. He wore the livery of the Church. _ _ . _</p>
        <p>The Rev. Wilfrid Eghazit, however, had not sought the appointment because of any inordinate love he bore the prisoners. It meant additimial emolument, and this he faithfully earned by s punctilious observance of his' duties, and strict attention to business. There was business in his voice, in his short, jerky manner, in his efildied list of hackneyed interrogations. He should have been an insurance agent or a shop-walker. He- had originally intended himself for tha law, but want of funds precluded this. The Church was cheaper to enter and gave quicker and More certain returns. S.P.Q.R. would have been Mr. Egham's motto if he had been in the drapery trade. A word of sympathy, a kindly smile, a kindly touch, an encouraging word, these did not always bring quick returns, and he did not deal in them. He knew Martin well, for he had offered regula- tion consolation when he was under sentence of death; btit No. 35 was a new personage, and had to be dealt with according to regulation. He opened a note-book, like a journalist at a street accident, and snapped out: "Your age? Don't answer if you object." "Twenty-four." "Parents living ? " "No." "What religion ? " "Is it really necessary ?" asked Fenwick. "I am Fenwick, you know. You saw me in the condemned cell."</p>
        <p>"-Yes, yes, I'm aware of it. But my duty is to put the usual queetions. Do you object to answer?"</p>
        <p>"I'm a Protestant, if that information is of use, and I have—"</p>
        <p>I"Prot." wrote the cleric, adding "C. E." "I think that will do for the present." he snapped out in his businesslike manner, and then, with the air of a shop/Ilan giving a little extra weight in, he added: "I hope and trust you recognise that prison discipline is a goodly. thing. What saith the wise man of Scripture : Despise not thou correction, oh, my son—' " But sick of the cant and humbug of it all Martin hadlurned away in disgust, and the cleric closed - his homily abruptly and left the cell. "An obstinate case, No 35," he Said to a warder outside. "Dead in trespasses and sins." But the warder in question, who was by no means a bad fellow at heart, knew the Rev. Wilfrid Egli= well, and detested his manner quite as much as the new "lags" invariably did. The fact thst Martin had resented the Rev. Eghant'i methods merely predisposed him in the prisoner's favour.</p>
        <p>remainecl to be seen whether Martin would become worldly wise with the wisdom of the prison cells of Henlea, which consisted of playing the humble and contrite hypocrite in response to the Rev. Egham's administrations, and thereby secur- ing whatever favours the reverend gentleman might have at his disposal. There were other formalities to be observed. A medical paamination revealed the fact to a humane medical officer that Martin, though his muscles were well developed, was paused to manual toil, and as his was a life sentence, he commenced with what was considered light labour. This consisted of cleaning and polishing his cell floor, polishing the tin mug from which he., drank,,and the plate from which he ate, and in picking oakum. Picking oakum amongst the old-timers in H. M. prisons has developed into a skilled operation, almost a fine art, but to the uninitiated the task of shredding the hard, tarred rope, with no help but that afforded by an old rusty nail, is a formidable one. _ _</p>
        <p>Martin, seated ou his bed, hammered the rope upon the bedpost and the door again and again without producing much appreciable difference. He tore at it with his fingers and naiLs_until the blood came. It was close' and stiffing in the cell, for he had worked himself into a per- spiration at his task, and, above all, he longed to speak to someone. Solitary confinement is diametrically opposed to our gregarious instincts. It is a torture of the mind. Day after day passed, 3et only once did a word 13%18E4 Ills lips. It IVILS on the second day, when.he ventured to say "Good- morning " to the warder, who visited him with his food.</p>
        <p>The warder fired him with a stony stare. "You know the regulations," he said. "You as nothing here until you're spketi to. If I catch you speaking again, I'll report you."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-04-26_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>LADDER OF GITILT.</head>
        <p>THE LADDER OF GITILT.</p>
        <p>BY ITLYSi ROGERS, Author of "For Love and the Queen," "Wedded in Mystery," &amp;a</p>
        <p>SYNOPSIS OF PRECEDING CHAPTERS: Douglas Low, madly in love with Grace Hendon, daughter of a neighbouring fanner, attempts to murder his cousin Martin Fenwick. His aim, however, un- accountably fails, and Squire Fenwick, his uncle, is the victim instead. The deed was witnessed by an old hag who has a grudge against the Fenwicks, and who accuses Martin of the murder. Martin is tried and found guilty.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER VI. ENTERING WON PROBATION. Ile was to live. The thought was overpowering. What • a wonderful, beautiful, glorious thing life appeared to him, even a life spent within prison walls In the fulness of our health, unfettered, with the joys of light and nature .and friendship surrounding us we give little thought to the glorious privilege of being alive. But in the depths and darkness of our despair. I when our earthly moiftents can be correctly calcu- lated, when the long farewell is on our lips, when in th 2 fulness of our lives the tape-measure of our existence , has unaccountably- been foreshortened, how sweet is a respite, however brief. Think of what it meant to Martin Fenwick. Life •to him wan hope. Ho viewed the circumstances as the direct interposition of Providence, and the first step towards the full expositio4, of his complete innocence. It gave time a chance of revealing the real perpetrator of the hateful crime for which he had, come so near suffering the last penalty, and he had great faith in God and in tiMe. ' Pryke, his , solicitor, would work for him. Money from the , estate should be forthcoming for that, purpose. How willingly would he brace himself to the convict life,before him. He longed to be at it, to do something. Suddenly a• key scrunched in the lock, and the gaoler who had attended him throughout and had received frequent tips for kindly attentions, entered. "Follow me,4' he growled. Martin obeyed the rasping voice and followed the gaoler through a tangle of 'corridors. At length they stopped. The gaoler thrust his 'key into another lock. The door swung open and an empty cell was revealed. It was smaller and less com- fortable than the one in which he had hitherto been placed. "Step in." "Am I going' to stay here ? " asked Martin. "Ask no questions, but do as you're told," snarled the man; "and when you are required'to speak be respectful, that's all, or it'll be the worse for you," and the fellow turned on his heel, slamming the door behind him and making the corridor echo with its clatter. Martin sat dmin upon the plank bed that was stretched beside the wall, and wondered why there should be such a change in the man, for he had not yet realised his proper position. So long as the law purposed robbing him of his life he was regarded as a human being; now he must come to regard himself as a mere chattel, a machine desti- tute of feeling, lacking in all that is human, an existent being whose aspirations must be quenched, whose body, will, mind, must be brought into abject subjection. He had yet to learn that he had dropped from the jaws of death merely to fall into a living•grave. • He had, in point of actual fact, commenced the most terrible period of his life, the nine months probation in the local gaol which every prisoner undergoes to be made meet for the society of the herd of lawbreakers he is afterwards to join at one or other of the great convict establishments. The rthal for the day was not yet over.. Another warder later in the afternoon led himl forth and supervised the order of the regulation' bath, a regulation which Martin, unlike so many criminals, really enjoyed. After that he was led naked and shivering into an apartment where a large, full-stuffed bag lay uponjlte floor. "TM it apt," coiereendedWthe warder, and Ufirtin obilted. • , A quantity of roughly-constructed clothing of light colour, embellished all over with representa- tions of the Government arrow, tumbled out upon the floor. "Now then, just fit yourself into one o them suits," Rata the warder. t yen to a labouring man used to the wearing of hard-earned corduroy such an attire, besmattered with the hall-stamp of roguery, could not but prove hateful. Martin, reared in comparative luxury, accustomed to dress well, felt the indignity all the more keenly. Yet it was futile to protest. He hesitated a moment, but the wardergrowled: "Now then, hurry uP. I can't" Waste all day over this," and he"went down on his !lanai' and knees and commenced rummaging among the vile clothing fo. something that Am near his fit. Most. of,theolleits were far too small; but at length Martin tfoutid one that would do and &amp;tiled t `ArraYed •in the short ' jacket and knickerbockers Of ,some deceased, or discharged prisoner, with vrough stockings which chafed his • sensitive feet, and a pair of boots that were two' sizes too big for' him and -made of leather stiff and unpliable, he presented- but a sad and sorry - contrast to the Martin Fenwick *hone trim aware:nee- set a-tea-11ton for half-therstylish and well-toeclo young:fellows in the county. But Partite! ' indignities were 'at hand. First he was rneaiured ' and weighed, mid the particulars entered in the prison-book together with divers not concerning the colotir of his eyes, and various idmitifications which might be expected to serve future purposes.' He was recolired also to pose with his hands in the air, palms sliewing, like a Yankee traveller "held up" by robbers.' This was for the purpose of having his portrait taken, to be added to the prison gallery of crime. The barber, too, claimed his attention, and, without undue finesse, quickly denuded his head of every particle of hair save a mere stubble' so short that it could not be plucked by the fingers, and to conclude his operation shaved off every vestige of the handsome moustache which had been such a distinguishing characteristic of his features. Finally he was informed that he would hence- as No. 35. forth be known "Now No. 36," said the warder who had bestowed his rough attendance upon him during all these preliminary operations, "you can get back to your cell." Mai tin preceded his gaoler, who saw him safely inside the asphalted cubicle he was to occupy. "Slain your door, and don't forget it every time yon enter your, cell," was the command, and rtin did as he was ordered. By this slamming did the practised ear of the warder tell, without always following to the door himself, that the prisoner was safely housed. Never had he felt' so utterly debased and humiliated as now, when he stood alone in the centre of his cell clothed in his convict garb—no longer a man but a number. 'Not even the sentence of the judge ringing with its fearfel doom produced the feeling of degradation he now felt. His birth- right of manhood had been stolen from him. Had lie been a criminal indeed, he would have been a worse criminal that moment than ever in his life before. Ile felt that he could bear the prison fare—he had partaken of one meal already under the new regime—he felt that he could tolerate the seclusion—at the moment it was welcome—he was not rebellious against the prison discipline, but the clothes, the number, the denudation of his dis- tinguishing cliaracf eri sties, these, comparative trifles to the old "lag" who has less sentiment regarding such matters, weighed heavily upon his sensitive nature. After spending hours cogitating upon his misery and thinking of Grace, he stretched himself upon his hard bed to spend the night, but, except in fitful slumbers, No. 35 found no sleep. Once he woke suddenly frem an uncertain doze to find -a stream of- light playing upon him. Were it not that he was blinded by the glare he would have seen that the light 'came from a lantern held at an aperture at the top of the door, and he would have beheld a pair of eyes gazing at him as he lay. It was the night warder going his rounds, 'as he after- wards surmised by hearinn, the regular footsteps along the corridor. He had been' nightly whilst lying under sentence clf death; but the warder whose duty it was had been a man of care- ful feeling and kindly instinct, and had been mind- ful of the fact that the prisoner's nights on earth were few, and made them u comfortable and un- disturbed as possible.</p>
        <p>Martin roe after a restless night, and, obeying Aqtriietions shOuled from Outside, rolled up the hiica• mattress and elaned and polished the floor ~f his cell. ,Scarcely had he done so when the door was thrown open and a man,: whose features :101d at once that he was not a warder,- was tish.red his first eie4ion el:l:fester] itho he was.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-04-26_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>CHAPTER VII. TIM TAPPING UPON 'tilt WALL.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER VII. TIM TAPPING UPON 'tilt WALL.</p>
        <p>Once a day the men took exercise in the yard. i They walked n single file, with fixed distances bet Ween each man, round and Anita the gravel path, warders 'being on the watch to prevent all"com- munications, yet some of-thit old hands succeeded in.daying the regulatiodi; and &amp;rrying on.quite a brisk correspondence, slight limp for hilf-a. cleen steps was a recognised way of indicating,the length of term to be six years; another way of communicating was by the blue handkerchief which every man had to bring into the exercise yard with him, and leave trailing oitt,of his pocket so that the warders could gee he had it. The number of times the handkerchief was removed from the pocket indicated certain stock- questions, such as: "Any toke to spare?" "I'm going to sham to fetch. the farm. Will you go for it too ? " "Fetching the farm" meant getting into that prisoner's paradise, the hospital. There the rigorous prison conditions were relaTed—talking was allowed, and the food was hafinitely better. The hospital at llenlea usually had half its beds occupied by healthy, hardened scoundrels, who had learnt the art of having fits, or of otherwise shamming sickness in order to "fetch the farm," where they escaped prison discipline, and had leisure and opportunity for the planning of future law-breakings. By similar signs, a fellow "lag," initiated in the signals, would make response, hut, of course, to those like Martin Fenwick, who had never had previous experience of the inside of a larison, the silence of the exercise-yard was as unbearable as that of the cell. For five minutes' free conversation with either warder or prisoner, Martin, in common with many others, would have, sacrificed a day's meals, precious though the 'limited quantity of coarse food was. Still, to see the faces, or rather the backs—for the convicts in the yard never met quite face to face —of friends in distress was something of a relief, and Martin used to long for the coming of the warder to release him for exercise.</p>
        <p>-- Upon these occasions he was generally preceded by a little prematurely-aged fellow from the next cell. 31artin began to feel a particular intereit in this individual from the fact that each night lifter lights were out, he kept him company by tapOng upon the wall. These taps appeared to have iv:mid- thing of significance in them, for they were repeated M regular intervals, and there was evident meaning in the number of them.</p>
        <p>One day, as they were returning from the exercise- yard, Martin, who had just crossed theihreshold, and for the moment was out of sight of the warder following, felt something thrust into his hand from behind. It was a piece of paper rolled up into a pellet. At the same time a voice whispered in his ear:</p>
        <p>"Put it into your mouth, and read afterwards." Quick as thought, Martin slipped the pellet into his mouth, and it lay snug and secure beneath his .onoue until he had entelod his cell and slammed the door behind him. 11n, eager with curiosity, he took out the pellet and unrolled the paper'. The words were almost illegible, written appar- ently with charcoal. And the message was a short one. This was all of it:</p>
        <p>"One rap, A; two, B; three, C." A great wave of joy filled Martin at this unlooked- for communication, for he knew in an instant that this was the key to the rapping on the wall The terrible monotony of his silent existence would at any rate he broken. Ile ate the paper, and longed for night to cone and the warders to cease their rininds thit his'lneighbour might open conversation. No. 48 did not waste time. No sooner had the Yisitina warder's steps died away along the corridor, than a commenced his strange telegraphy. His first anxiety was in connection with the message; but Martin set his mind at rest by assuring him that he had eaten it, and in return asked for the solution of what to him was a baffling mystery. - How had No. 48 secured paper and pencil to write ' the message? “Nothing simpler," was the reply, slowly tapped back on the dividing wall. "Last, page of Malachi and the bread-skewer." These terms might be unintelligible enough to us. but they were retain enough to Martin. He WWI</p>
        <p>quite autneientiy initiated in prison regulatioxis to grasp the meaning. Always after this he made a point of searching the Bible supplied to him in big cell, and, though during his prison career he had opportunities of inspecting several copies of the Divine Book, yet in only one did he find the last page of "Malachi," and not once the last of "the Revelation." For these pages, as every old " lag" knows, have a portion of their surface entirely blank, and notepaper is a luxury supplied but at rare intervali, and then only the prison sheet with the prison regulations written thereupon. Ingenuity, too, had supplied the pencil. It is required by the regulations that each prisoner shall have a certain number of ounces of bread each day, and when the weight happens to be short, a piece is skewered on to the deficient loaf. The skewer, with its end burnt in the gas jet, had made No. 48, and many a man before and after, a most useful substitute for a pen. Late into the night the two men cony,rsed, with the wall as their medium. It was a slow and tedious occupation at first, and a constant watch had to be kept for the warder's footsteps, for detection meant separation and bread and water for a month at least.</p>
        <p>No. 48 had a sad story to tell. "I am slowly but surely going mad." he said. "This terrible silence is killing me. Thank God, I have at last found a means of communicating with my fellow-man." "How long have you been .here ? " Minced back Maren, a process which occupied the greater par of the minutes to accomplish. \ "Seven rqonths," was the reply, "and. I *Mild rather die than spend them over again. I'M in for forgery. What's yours?" Martin shuddered at the question, but he answered boldly: "The accusation against me is that of murder; of which I am as innocent as you are." "And how did you escape the rope?" asked the other, not one whit punctilious about holding con- versation • with a convicted murderer. He had intended to reply • that innoeence was a standing characteristic of that establishment; but he checked the observation for fear' that No. 35 might be unduly sensitive, and end the acquaintance. Martin told him of the marvellous escape he had bad.</p>
        <p>"Marvellous," he said, "I never heard of any- thing to approach it. Suppose you regard it as direct intervention of God, and so on:"</p>
        <p>"I am profoundly convinced of it," replied Martin. "My full innocence will yet be proved." "Well, I call it remarkable luck. What has God done for me ? " he asked. And the heart of No. 48- was full of bitterness and hopelessness. "Look at me! Brought up in the Sunday-school, then a Sunday-school teacher, and after that a local preacher. Religious environment all around me. Got married, children came—four of them. Then just when things should have been going all right, everything went wrong. My employer took to the racecourse, and gambled his business away- I was one of the first to go to lessen the expenses. Close upon forty years of age, it took me five months to get into employment again. My new position only lasted six months; and then again, through no fault of my Own, I was again sent hunting for work, with a terrible arrear of debt rapidly increasing at home. When work at length came, at a miserable thirty shillings a week (I had been receiving three pounds ten in my first place), I was so far behindhand in my payments that, driven one day to desperation by the threat of the bailiffs, I forged a cheque for five pounds—a. miserable five pounds. • "Of course, I was scotched. It is always the novice who is caught. A shrewder, more experienced man would 4,1,9 got clear off with a thousand, for the opportunity was simplicity itself. But lure I am, as the reatilt of it., Two years' bard, and the wife and • youngsters in the workhouse. God knows, I was tempted and tried enough before-I did the deed. All my previous good character went for nothing with the judge. If anything, my religious position but heightened the offence, in his eyes. Now they have made a gaol-bird of me; and when. I get out I shall be hounded from place to place by the detectives, as though it would not be hard enough for me to get work at my age without their interference." • •</p>
        <p>Martin was greatly interested in his new acquain- tance, and , promised, if he obtained his liberty, , to see that an opportunity of employment was pnt. in ' his way; , a'proniaiss Which No, 48 smiled indulgently . over. lie knew only • toe Well , that the earliest thane&amp; of No. •,35 seeing liberty.would, mot be until after nearly twentylears ; Itard he 'himself, in? his present morbid ankiiistraught'Condition, looked for little, hope:rat that time of bis life. , Night after might the two .;,e,onrersed by rapping•• upon the walls.: and each quickly grew proficient, at it, so that ailiange of ideas was far more rapid than upon the first night. The'friendship had proved most opportune to No. 48. Little by little, under the cheering cOunselof Martin, his pessimism began to vanish, and a Wild longing for freedem and another chance in life'took possession of him. To him the reprieved murderer in the next cell had come .as, •an ang4l,og light. and the,,,darkneiS,,,, the hopelessness; the • bitterness was slowly land slimly , vanishing from,his soul, and the Madness from his •Itut one.night, Whilst No. 48 was rapping out a message to Kerlin, there was ,a suddim crunch of 14 " 4T4'.14d; , 48's , cell; and tlui ripping suddenly ,mease4:,: TW,fleor tiew Open,,,mnd,a, rough, grating voicaex!qheinied,: ; 'clifislioat',lSo!this is , yet little game is it? It's : usk , ,Tibeilold,ii,,Mteeze to last long without ;y; ''f tJjetsg:Copieltqmilg Ali .ii;lep, . . p,4; i . 48: ctit4ed:;nift liitot.-Ae._`,'Orriclor',. and ','ls couple of 'seconds leiterwards 3cart.in'e door was --ornetir-enel--her-tee—greti--teken-hef,t. ore—the-ohief warder. result of their interviCiiiiiikalehgence of bread and.waterfor a mouth and each prisoner 'to be removed to a separate nell. "Good-bye,2inriendif shouted No. 48 as he was being ,led 'away. "Silence!" roared the warder. "Give him an extra daily dose of the crank, and make his term a bread and water six weeks," com- manded the chief Officer. • "Then Twit? speak," cried No. 48, now losing all cominand 'Of ,timselU and betraying only too surely those signs ,of lapse of mind of which he had assured Martin. "I will speak; curse you-:_." But a hand was thrust over his mouth,,and three warders seizing him by the neck and wrists he was , quickly hurried out and pitched into the celrappor- tioned him ' Martin:,liad Said nothing, but his blood had boiled with, indignation' at what had passed before his eyes, for the warders had added vindictive spite to the performance of their duties. The man who had stopped No. 48's mouth had, in reality, made ' his action a pretext for giving the man a violent blow. The prisoner was but of slight and delicate build, and the three burly' warders who had hurried him 1 off had twisted his arms until his cries of pain-had Ireverberated along the corridor. It was brotal, debasing, demoralising. The system was wrong to start with. The method of enforcing that systexa would have been called criminal anywhere outside of a prison. Martin wondered . Wh'ether‘ the governor knew of such things. For some weeks Martin did net See his late neighbour. One day, however, he noticed lin 'apparent new-comer, a cowed,"dejected, shifty-eyed little man awn. the 'prisoners in the exerciseryard: ,:A., seCond ;,glance told him who he was. It was. No. 4E14 dint he had changed treendously'since he last .Saiv him: The awful silence, combined with the poor fare and the useless daily slavery at the crank, was assuredly driving him mad. The drirelling leer of rapidly approaching lunacy flitted at 'times , across his features; at the next moment it would be changed into the deep, furtive cunning of a low and crafty hatred. • .</p>
        <p>No. 48 shewed no sign of recognition either that day or the day after, but on the third day he seenied to wake as from a deep lethargy. It was s. terrible awakening, awful in its results; for, withor• a moment's hesitation, lie ribbed open-armed acro-s the 371.1 to his friend crying aloud in his gladness. He did not reach 3elartin, however, for a warder stepped quickly across with drawn cutlass, and struck the unfortunate wretch a blow with thetthst side of his weapon full across the head. No. 48 dropped to the stones like a log. i Martin was too full of indignation to restrain</p>
        <p>himself this ,time. • "You • brute l You cowardly bn.itel" he muttered. But. he was heart\ and the next instant ,! coaple olf,stoi:ely warders had. flung themselves upon- him. Tre was promptly led back to the cells. No. -48 was now in the throes of a paroxysm of ins4ity. His strength 'fe.mporarily intensified by the raging imaniac within him, he suddenly threw off the clutch of the gaolers, who perhaps had s‘knewhat -released their hold now that he was :Within the narrow confines of the stone-walled . Corridor, and in a moment he was darting like a hare along the passage. It all, happened before Martin's eyes, the sharp struggle, the release, the chase, and then, as he wondered upon the uselessness of it all, No. 48 suddenly stopped at a point where a flight Of stone .tens. guarded at the ton by an iron railing. led to</p>
        <p>dungeons Detow. The hunted man cast ond demoniacal look back at his purseers, and thell' 1 ' wlth a mocking grin upon his face he leaped do . .. railing. A warder made a frantic grab at him WI , 11218 too late. With a sickenstoe, thud the wretcnoes. • num fell head foremost on to the flags fifteen foe" below. . Now that their systematic regulation cruelty log accomplished its bitter end, the warders threw og somewhat of their officialism, and for the WI ' became human. They raised the moaning sada' with an attempt of tenderness, and bore him off ?.° . the hospital. Reason claimed the remaining moments of 104 life, and he moaned piteously for the comradeshiP ' of his prison friend, No. 35—so piteously.and olk continuously in fact, that at length Martin • taken to the hospital ward. "Thirty-five," said the dying man, "God groat' you may get out of this. I'm going soon." There was no reason to suppose otherwise. Idi. ' wasted features would have told that tale even . without the dreadful scalp wound that was bringing him to a quicker, surer end. "There is a wife and fopr youngsters in the work' ' house at Tottenham," he groaned, "without ilt, friend-in-the-world:- You'll keep an eye on them? . "I promise, if ever I'm free," said Martin. "Tell them," he said, "that I died repentant. , believing as I preached. You'll tell them that. because everybody said I was a hypocrite, and cast' a slur on them as though they had done wrong.' And tell them that befpre God it was the oily time I'd done wrong. There were other forgerieel bit, 1 I swear I had no hand in them. You'lli ell them? "I promise," said Martin. - 1 "Good-bye!" he said, endeavouring to stretd, 1 forth his hand. Martin took it firmly in his. "Good-bye, "he said. , The dying man's hand tightened on his own, Martin stood holding it until it grew quite cold. , A great wave of loneliness swept over his soul.' The solitude of the existence before him mad" thoughts of death sweet even to Martin. But across' the memory of the sad vista of his reflections stole, the sweet image of a womanly girl, with brown curls and tender blue eyes, and the memory of , voice which said: "I will wait for you, Martill;;''r even though my hair turn grey in the waiting. And once more a ray of hope peeped into his soul, ' and he determined to live on, to pray on, to hoprte, on, until time and God above should work out hii•r release.</p>
        <p>- • "Here, come on! You can't stand about herr, for ever.. Let go. There's no use in holding-l3' hand now. Can't you see he's dead?" Martin turned and followed the warder . back M. the solitary-cell, with its crank and the several.; conditions which his defiance of regulations ts'. brought upon him.</p>
        <p>To be- continued.)</p>
        <p>The Death of a Warrington Man in Prison. n quest was condo( Si r arigewaya Go Manehrator, on Smuniay, by J F Price, coon' coroper, relative to the ueath of John Winstanl,</p>
