        <p>.)NS OF WOMEN USE CEJTICURA SOAP for beautifying the skin, the stopping 431- Ing hair, for softening and whitening red, ti°° hanc:s, for annoying irritation, ijilinnima- ann ehatings too free or offensive perspir. I° ‘Ant4le,r, 'for ulcerative Weakilefit3, and m "" „ Y sanative antiseptic purposes which 11, 3' ontrreat thetneelves to women and esie- toil Mothers, and for all the purposes of the bath and price nursery. tlhcraistisiz postpaid 4.1. ' e. nv;t</p>
        <p>directors naturally expec'ted to see some results. If sada man only succeeded in saving sixpence a day ia materialthe aggregate would amount to over £3.000 in the coarse of a year. That showed them what avoid be done by individual effort. 'they might think that a little bit here and a little hit there did no matter, but. that was not so. It was this additios of small I avings which. totalled up to such a large sum when added together. By attending to these small details toe work- men were only giving something in return for what the company was paying out to them in bonuses. It bad keen agreed upon -by tha principal _ firms in the Geode to make a reduction in prices, and they had got trO make the beat of it, and endeavour to save what they would fail to. get in the Way of.: prices. Passing how from the firm' r sayings to the employes' savings 'Mr Richmond said in •his profit sharing speech last vear he was anxious to! impress -upon them the staventeges of endeavouring to save a little rale , weerias ask, d those 'who had saved £2 during the year Ito put their hands up, and, if he remembered rightly, , there were only about a d, zon who put their hands up. (Laughter.) A man told him last year that families took a good deal to keep. He daresay they remembered that, (Laughter.) It was quite true that they did take a good deal to keep, but ti-os ought to save a litt'e It was the continual persistent , if mt which amounted to a great deal ia_the end. ytist bad arranged for a Govern- ment official from the Post Office Savings Bank 'Depart— ment to attend at the works that evening, during the profit sharing distribution, to receive any awn they might be disposed to deposit in the Post Office Savings Bank He trudei that advantage would te taken of ; the opportunity thug afforded for saving the spare cash 1 they would find in the envelopes, and having opened an acceunt, betwould,advise them to add something every week., If they .did that, they would bless the day that profit sharing! Was started. It was no use whatever the cOmpany might do, if the men themselves did not act Op to it. All they (Masers. R.chmonds) were doing was to put them in the right way, ti show them a pat along-which; if, they trod., they would not dread sickness or tboiworkhreuse. •The.directors were aturieUs toseei every roan prosperous, boned in a oomfoirkhle, home, and a future to look forward to which Aid not terminate in th , workhense. They could noa do more than they were doing, and he hoped ttoe employes would , follow the advice gives". (Applause). I Mr J A RANSONIR Slid be was sure he was expressing the opinion of eVeryone present when he welcaraed Mr Richmond among them. (Hear, hear.) Mr Rich- mond was a very busy Man. He had many calls upon him, his time was fully occupied.and consequently they did not see 80 much of him as they would like to do. Be thought they might look upon the profit sharing scheme and:the business as two very fine children. The business tisrl get on pretty well. Mr Rimpironto : It has cut its teeth. (Laugh.er.) , Mr. Rasseittitlcontinuing) said the profi--shering waal no pretty 'well able to-walk. (Renewed laughter,) It depSndedamritithe employes how these, childree were going to turn , out, ,They must do, their very best to exert their talents io ',Whatever department they might bs engaged ja,and conduct the business on the cheapest possible lines, conducive to good work. If the business' went wrong, the profit-sharing svnitl.l go wrong. ! IDS woald like to endorse Mr Richmond's remarks about laying for themielves. Certainly there were many claims upon their wecer, but they ought to remember that old age was seining on. (Hear, hear.) Most of them were young men and in the prime of life, hut there woeld come a time when they might not he able to vroik for a living, and, if they did not want to fall upon friends, or the poor rates of the borough, they would require to have a little nest-peg of their own. The dividends from the shares which they were now taking up; would be something to assist them. Ile was glad that the rye:At-sharing scheme was still very suceesatni. And that it was getting stronger every year. Ha had pleasure in proposing a vote of thanks to Mr Richmond' for his presence. (Applause). Mr DAVY, the works manager, Seconded the propna:. tion. He remarked that the firm had allthe elements to make it a success. They had up-to-date men and up-to date machinery and the two combined made for success AAlereve all, with a man like Mr Richmond at the head of Affairs, if they did not make the business a success, they *au% be a lot of duffers. (Laughter). ',Mr- HUGHES, in supporting the resolution, said profit 'Awing was a great experiment, and no one realised it 1 more than he did. Be combated theidea at the .outset of the soheme,but Mr Richmond said he was determined to tryit for five years. Be (Mr Hughes) realised what a serious thing it was for the firm to undertake each a scheme. The responsibility now rested with the em- ployes to make it a great:success. (Applause). 4 Mr. THORNTON, as the manager at the Stratford ttlborks, said they at Stratford looked to Warrington to -give them some good advice. I; was pleasant to see Warrington going forward so rapidly, and the Stratford man wished them further success in the future. Be eupportei the vote of thanks. (Applause): Mr. GROROE BILLINOTON aud Mr. JOsern FORD OW supported the proposition on behalf of the workmen. --MY. FORD said he could assure !Me. Richmond that from what he had seen of the men at work they endeavoured to prevent waste and turn out a good ' article. He believed every man had the welfare of the firm at heart. Daring the past year he had been absent the greater part of the time in South Africa. He was agreeably eurprioed,on returning to work,to find that he was to participate in the profit-sharing as though he had been at work. (Applause). L./ The propo4Don was enthusiastically carried, the men diiiging "For he's a joiligeod fellow." . Mr. thoseacien,-Itt acknowledging the compliment, Said he 'appreciated"thOptet that, as he went din, d;he men and the staff, be 'knew he was Mixing araMibligstgat *lose who wished him well. It made him feel that be bad not lived his life in vain. If this good feeling (pasted amongst all workmen and employers there would pa no trouble about strikes. It was nezessary for ileastere and men to understand each other, and to have (..11 ' little sympathy-one for the ,other. If .men wanted to , get the best pf everything it created friction and they would not ger_,yhiit they wanted. It was the sae _When be mestere "derired to hate the bier of eiterythiart. 'lt /as only by endeavouring to fairly share the fruits of labour, capital, and risanagemerit that pros- perity could be aresurei, and the work carried on in an amicable and satisfactory manner. (Applause.) le supposed be would have the pleasure of meeting ins at Douglas in June next. They were going to ar- ..uge to have the sea quits smooth. (Laughter.) They would remember. that when he was si)eaking there twelve months ago they were celebrating the relief of llilafekftit.' -"this year they had got to pay for it. '4l.isughteKl 'He told thern,a year, ago,that the workmen who had gone to the front would' participate in the Profit sharing scheme as'though the! had been working and they had carried that out,because they had been do- ing their share for the rest of them, at the front. (Hear. bear.) In conclusion Mr Richmond pointed out that he rind had under consideration the inauguration of a i:superarinuation scheme, whereby a payment of 61 a }week up to the age of 65 wouti secure VW each work- man a' pension of from 7s fftl to 101 a week as long as he lived... (Applause.) He had seen actuaries. insurance managere and others on the Matter but be bad Slot been i Isle to devise a satisfactory scheme: He mentioned the latter to show them that they were not only thinking profit-sharing but were always endeavouring to im- srove the conditions of labour. If, later on, he could Oome before them with any satisfactory scheme on the lines suggested he would be only too pleased. (Applause). . Envelopes containiug share certificates and dividends Were then distributed and the interesting proceedings terminated.</p>
        <p>COD LIVER OIL. Its Many 'Uses and the Best Way to Take it. Statist*cs show that cod-liver oil,though an oldfashioned remedy, is lislargelv consumed as ever. Cod-liver oil,however,does not find its way entirely to the market in its pure and simple state. It is in the form of the agreeable emulsions—like that of Scot's—that it is more often placid before the public. It is an interesting fact that medical science has never discovered any substance to take the place of cod-liver oil, One would be inclined to fancy that the rxhaustrvg research and experiments of tho last fifty years would have resulted in the aban- donment of one of the earliest methods of cure for general weakness and disease, but the fact is quite the contrary. Cod-liver oil is more generally prescribed to- day by physicians, and more generally understood and employed by the public than ever before. We attribute much of the popularity of cod-liver oil as a household remedy to a world-renowned preparation which, at one stroke, overcame all the objections formerly made by patients, who complained tither that they could not take the oil on account of its taste, or that it disturbed their digestion and thus caused distress. The prepara- tion to which we refer is nnown as Scott's Emulsion, which offers the highest grade medicinal oil in a form -that is already emulsified, and completely disguises its taste.</p>
