        <p>For idle hands to do. (Laughter). El.s looked up .in Mr Jolley as one of those , idle hands. (Renewed Isu4.lver). He was a man of ability, and they must find him nom ihing to do. He would got into mischief if the Board did not find him something to do. He had discharged his duties with conspicuous abi sty and had given satisfac- tion to all of them. (El ,ar, hear) Mr BALL -seconded, and Dr PATTER.ON and others supported the cation, which was put to the meeting and carried unanimously. Toe OUTMAN, in reply, said he particularly desired to thank the proposer and seconder, and the members of th Board for this cordial manner in which the resolu- tion had been received. Hs had Tiite exoec7ed that Dr i Patterson, who had bcen senior vice-chairman during the past two year, w ii I have been olecled t ths chair. Is theref ire, gave him 4 iditional gratification to notice that Dr Patterson supported the res Aut• on as Cordially as any other member. In view of what f sll from Mr Knowles, he ought to cay that there wPre many (1,108- tlorli before the B oard in a p arti 411 y advanced state .which should be pushed forward, and which he hoped would b ourod ter ward io a siatisfac..ory manner. Re thought the spirit of o' divine discontent " should pre— vail, and urge th ,m to higher attainments than they hid yet reached. There was room for impr oveine it in more directions than one. (Hear, hear). TEIE VICE-CH ItRAtiN.</p>
