        <p>Mr Pitmen; said it was usual at that meeting to appoint a small committee to look out for a suitable site for new Door law offices. The coalmine had found it a very difficult matter to secure a suitable site, They had one or two in view but they had not ben able to Come - any concUilon. lie f ormally moved that the site -Aeotion committee be clnposied of Moises A J Jolley, Dr Patterson, William Houghton, G Pilling,and Thomas Halton: . Mr Btr.caucut said the committee had not dons very I much good in the past. The counlittee had not preseated a report Of their proceedings, and 'unfortun- ately this was a matter which needed earneit attention. The Lund Governm-nt Board and the Registrar General were desirous that the Board should move in the matter</p>
        <p>of building new offices. Mr—firintiLvs theught It was inadvisable for the committee to act at Present in the matter. It would be better to wait until the Cerporato Put down their tramways so that they could select a site for offiees which would be near them. He did not think there was any necessity to harry the matter forward. ' He was in favour of a site comnilttee being appeisted but he did not wish to urge them on too much. The CHAIRMAN pointed out that the Rural District Council seat a requisition to the Board urging them to take certain action towards providing new offices. The committee had entered into negotiations in an informal way bet they had nothing definite to bring forward. In the Clerk's report on the estimates two years age, the Clerk pointed out that a few years ago a discutsion arose as to the advisability of providing central poor law offices for the Union as the Warrington Union was at a considerable disadvantage by the present arrangement. It was then decided to appoint a committee to select a site, and the necessity had since much increased. The Rural Council recommended the advisability of urgent steps being taken. Mr KNOwtEs said at the time that resolution was passed. the Rural Council used to sit in a little room used by Mr Sutton as an office. Since then the Rural C;uncil had removed to the late Highway Board's room and it was found a very convenient one, and the Rural Council seemed fairly content with it. The CLERK said personally he would prefer to !et the present offices suM;e for his time, but he must remind them that the ,accommodation wastaxed to the utmost. Another sate was needed in which to Itlep the deeds of the Union but there was no room for a. safe. The Registrar General was almoet sure to call upon the Board shortly to provide a new building for the purpose , Having that in view, the Board would probably con- sider the whole question. The Bites commktee as recommended by Mr. PILLING was appointed. Mr PILLINU then proposed a further resolution that in view of the difficulty attending negotiations for the purchase of a suitable sit for new union offices, that the committee be empowevd to take any action on behalf of the Guardians they may deem expedient, sub- ject to the consent of the Local Government Board and the Registrar General. Be contended that such a resolution was necessary in order to enable the com- mittee to do its wo,k. Be wag told only a few days ago that a gentleman who attended their present offices, , said "it was a di.grace not only to the Clerk but to the I Guardians that the business had to be conducted in such I a hole." Mr WILLTAM EIOUGHTON seconded the aesolution. Mr liamwdits said this was an important spending matter and he did not agree to placing the power of selecting a site.in the hands of four or five people. When the site was selected be wanted to have a word in it,</p>
