        <p>Ile therefore moved an amendment nat the com=ittee be empowered to Nele,lt se i aid reeominerid to the iard of Guardiams Cite for their comideration and 4pproval. Mr HICEMLN soconded the amendment.</p>
        <p>Mr FortstLAW thought the present offices would suffice f •.fie or-senr. He was yore much against the offices ot toe Uoion being seoaraed from the Workhouse. the H v FATHER rvir.sori stid he wished VI support the ameadm Re Lti no.-4ht the present wis a ,time for the exercise, of economy seeing that the rates were increasing, Toe Rev I R MaTTIIEWs suggested that sites were getting scarce and more exp ,nsive.Ele was not in favour of spending money, bat if they wire to have these buildings the sooner the site was obtained, the better. Me HICKMAN said it was true that sites were getting scarce,but this committee had had two years to get one, and sites mast have been scarce two years ago or one would have been Ob:sined. In an important matter like this which meant the spending of a large sum of money , the Board ought to have some say in the matter of the site. It was nor the corn mi tee to rind a site, and submit it for the approval of the ard. He did not see iny , necessity to hurry the matter. They had pretty well in , hand at present and were spending an enormous amount of money. , Mr Homan. : floe mist important matter has not been mentioned.</p>
        <p>The CH&amp;IRDISN said Mr Houghton had ahead? spoken and was out ot order. Coutinuing, the Chairman, said he wanted 13 say award about the point of economy which had just ben meotioned. There was such a thing as trus economy and such a thing as false economy The difficulties that the committee had had to face were very great indeed. frie corn nitcee had not been idle, and he believed the B oard had a reasonable Mn aunt of confidence in tha good sense and business capacity of the members of the committee. Some of the Guardians were perhaps not aware of the fact that the Board had to provide office accommodation for the Superintendent registrar. If the Registrar General atated that the accommodation was insufficient they would have to go elsewhere. It must not be forgotten that the B lard was paving rent year by year and had nothing to show for It. Was it desirable to rent additinnal rooms ? With reference to the question of economy which had been raised, he did not see where the call for economy came in if they could provide poor la +I offic•s without an !addition to th 3 rates. If there had been any public ' extravagance in Warrington the Guardians were not open to the charge, if anytning, they as a Board could juscly claim to have been moo: econ Janina Toe , Guardians had not caused any increase of rates, the in. I cress's was Inc to other airthoritieS. ME WILLIADI TIOUGHTON asked what was the amount of rent paid by toe Guardians for poor law offices ? The CLERK : About £lOO 4 year. Mr-KNOWLES : Would it cost about £lO,OOO to build offices ? Mr PILLING O's no! M• KNOWLES : I was to! S that this new infirmary would not cost £10,030 or £20.000. Mr PILLING saw no reason to defer the question until the Corporation Tramways were laid, b 'Cc use it was in- tended to build the new offices in the centre of the town and therefore they were lure to be near the tram- way service. With reg,ard to Father Wilson's remark about the rates, he muit add that the Guardians had not increased the poor rate. That rate had never been chatoged at all, A vote was then takeo, and Mr Klowlea' amendment was carried. Mr HICHNUN said the committee would have to do the same as toe N 'won C orimit'ee and recommend a site for the acceptance of the Board. DEPU f A HON.</p>
