      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-04-26_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>SHOCKING ACCIDENT AT ST. . HELENS.</head>
        <p>SHOCKING ACCIDENT AT ST. . HELENS.</p>
        <p>Mishap to a Colliery Headgear.</p>
        <p>. A serious accident happened on Saturday afternoon at the Crop pit of the St. Helens Collieries, belonging to Yrothers, to a gang of men who were engaged changing the steel rope which winds coal and men ontnt thwpit. The result was that George Wood- ward, of 36, ESeter-street, aged twenty-eight, died soon after admiesion to the S. Helens Hospital, and a man named' Alfred Pr —igby was seriouily hurt. It appears that Woodward and Rigby were, aiong with other men, under the personal supervision of Mr Brittan, the underimanager, and Thomas Goulding, the surface foreman, engaged on the werk. The two nun were standing on the frame, seven or eight yards Wgh, near to the headgear, to guide the end of the old rope, which was being slowing unwound off the drum. When the "capping" of the rop,,,which had been fixed to the drum, came to the men they were about ta lift it over some bars, when it overcame them, and threw the frame over. As it was falling Rigby jumped off, and had his arm broken in three places, and inpred his back. Deceased held on to the falling erection, and was most aericusly injured internally by the woodwork. Dr Cotton was on the scene in a few minutes, and the two men were re moved to the hospital, where Woodward died.</p>
