      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-04-26_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>WIDNES DIVISION LIBERAL ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>WIDNES DIVISION LIBERAL ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>The General Election. A meeting of the above association was held on Mon- day evening at the Liberal rooms in Alfordr-street, for the purpose of electing the various commi.tees. Mr Eustace Carey occupied the chair and he was supported by Mr H B Shores and Mr Max Muspratt. The Fatter gentleman gave a very interes lug address on " Organ- ization." He said they had reached a point when facts should be looked in the face. Last October the Liberals , of the Widnes division sustained the nvst crushing de- feat during the history of the division. Ha thought it was the most crushing defeat rhac any industrial com- munity had received in the Liberal Interest. Jr. was no use going on in the same way as in the past. Toes? had ! to face the fact that the organization, excellent though it was on paper, and democratic tboug,b it was in idea, had entirely broken down. They wanted to dud out u here the fault lay and how it could be remedied. He wished to draw their attention to the fact that, at the late general election, the Liberals, taking the country as a whole, had lost ground and they should be able to unite in that part of the constituency for the or- ganization was on the most democratic lines. Out of a total of 400 representatives there were 200 in Widnes to whom were entrusted the fortunes of the party of the division. Representatives were also elected to l he Central Council who had to do the executive work of the divi- sion. How was it that such an excellent democratic institution, when election time came round, was not to be found in the active lines of the workers? The most excellent democratic system can do little if it had loat its vitality. Many Liberals showed a positive unwill- ingness to take part on the Liberal side. There was an inapre.sion that the election was run from Liverpool 'arid not from Widnes, and the Reform Club had sent a can- didate and that the gentlemen of Widnes would not have a candidate foisted upon them. The criticism was well founded so be asked why it was done. The only reason why the non-resident Liberals connected with this division took an active part and did work that ought to have been done by resident Liberals was because of the impossibility of getting resident Liberals to take part in the work they should do. It was merely a question whether the work was to ho dene or not. The non-residents were most anxious to see it broken down though they wouldbe always ready to assist in the future but not to take any initiative. The committee elected for the coming year would have the real problem to reconsider. It was, therefore, particularly important that in the election that evening no men should be put on simply because it was thought they ought to be ' on. This was perhapa a high ideal but it was a necessary see. An industrial place like Widnes should be radical. It was a sad sight to walk down Victoria-road and Waterloo</p>
      </div>
