      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-04-26_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <p>AI.</p>
        <p>THE successful "joint Nonconformity and Politics. sessions" of the Baptist andCongregatipnal Unions held this week in London mark another step in a movement towazds re-union, which has</p>
        <p>for some time been in progress among the Free Churches of the Kingdom. The Free and United Presbyterian Churches of Scotland have effected an "incorporating Union " making the two one and the first Moderator of the United Free Church,PairrarAL RAINY,WaS deservedly accorded an enthusiastic reception by the Congregational- ists and Baptists assembled in the City Temple on Tuesday. That the Union of- the Free Presbyter- ian Churches in Scotland has not barred the way to co-operation between them and the Established Church of Scotland, is evi- dent from the fact that the coming General Assembly of the Established Church is to be " overturned " for the appointment of a committee to promote co-operation, especially in the Mission field, with the United Free Church. The Estab- lishment, of course, stands in the way of any closer union than the term co-operation expresses. In England, the Episcopal Establishment is un- doubtedly a more formidable bar even to co- oneration with the Free Churches, than is the , more Democratic Presbyterian Establishment in Scotland. But there is no reason why the Eng- , glish lime Churches should not draw closer together, especially if they adopt the principle, recognised in the recent union in Scotland, of " allowing open questions," basing their relations on the essentials wherein they agree, while tolerating the non-essentials on which they differ. The Free Church Councils which have been established throughout the country, and the recent United. Missioe of the Free Churches afford other indications that this movement is bearing fruit. A similar movement towards re-union is in progress amongst the Wesleyan bodies, though hitherto no proposals for such an association of the Wesleyans and Con- gregationalists have been made, as that which has resulted in the joint sessions of the Congregational and Baptist Unions. Whatever may be the finer outcome of this movement it ought to help towards</p>
        <p>the re-habilitation of Nonconformist influence as a force making powerfully for righteousness in the State. It is mainly non-political, but it cannot wholly ignore the political basis of Nonconformity.</p>
        <p>The remark has often been made that Noncon-</p>
        <p>fortuity has in late years declined as a political force, and to that fact is in large measure</p>
        <p>attributable the weak position of Liberation:). to-day. The Nonconformists, as Mr. GLAD: sroNE more than Once ungrudgingly testi' fled, formed the great back bone of the Liberal party. The political Dissenter w5O the hardest fighter in the ranks of Liber.ilisrn, in the days when Amin JAMES and BurNEy, Biwa/</p>
        <p>and STOCKWELL BROWN and SPURGEON made the, pulpit's influence felt in the land. The decline re' Nonconformity as a political force in thee latter days is due to two main causes, the removal of re- ligious disabilities, and differences of opieien among Nonconformists themselves. The successee of Liberalism really proved its own undoing) bfi removing the grievances which fired the politica enthusiasm of many Dissenters. Then the flope Rule question came and split the ranks of 1011- conformity as it did the Liberal party, and 15t°.</p>
      </div>
