        <p>oconfor mists have had their differences overthe I Boer War—differences which have prevented, or at all events weakened, united political action atnnr-gst them. But the movement towards re- though mainly religious, should have its Influence in the pohtical sphere and tend to arouse more to activity in favour of wise and just v2ileY, the potent "Non-Conformist Conscience." kt his term is of ten used as a sneer, but of its historic conscience," to use Disraelic phrase, eral Nonconformity has no occasion to be Illhasied, and it may again prove a dynamic force till at shall influence and direct the entire course of ;gislation and National policy. The union of the ree Cuurches will, at all events, it is permissible O hope, prove beneficial to the Nation as well .as the Church in educating and developing a healthy tolerant and just public opinion on great questions of the day.</p>
        <p>The National Trade THE conditioe of trade is once more beginning ser- Mance Sheet. ioasly to exercise the minds qthe British public. With a Budget which im- iP°les burdens on two of our leading industries, with declining exports and railway traffics and with frequent reports of the increasing success of t,eige competitors in British markets, it was in- „i'table that some degree of concern should be elt as to Britain's industrial future. But weekly et, Monthly fluctuations are less reliable as indica- tlll:4lB of the state of trade than accurate returns c), longer periods and covering more completely the National activity in the domain of commerce till industry. The annual statement just pub- of the foteign trade of the United Kingdom 1,7 the past year does not confirm the pessimistic aCe which are being taken in certain quarters itia.clegh it is possi'ale to read in the figures special I ex 'cations of an unfavoureble character. Our eZts rose from £329,534,658 in 1899 to e4i373,751 in 1890; and the imports from 435,583 in 1899 to £523,075,163 last year, ele site of increase in both cases being s ery ;1114% Our total foreign trade in 1900 reached 8 immense sum of £877,418,917, which is greater 'lan in any previous year. These figures represent values only, and to some extent the increases are due to higher prices; but not only so, the volume of trade having also grown on both the import and' export side. The detailed figures of staple trades' _ars 1945. 14V011rIblOy eqd, son e cleclines, iq f9r in- etaUÔG, special. branches of both textiles 8,811 metal manufactures, are recorded in the export tables. But when we consider that .during the whole of last year S disastrous war was desolating part of our Ern- -1314 and during at least six months of the year °Jle of the best of our foreign merkets (the 1114°80 was also practically closed by war, while the Purchasing capacity of another of our great Possessions (India) was also seriously impaired by tarllble and pestilence, it is really remarkable that he aggregate figures of our commerce should anch ;vast increases. What troubles econo- of protectionist proclivities, however, is the elierease of what is called the trade balance against 4' that is, the excess of imports over exports. erently we imported last year nearly 170 tel:iMls more than we exported. By deducting tie- 4-exports of foreign and colonial merchandise th -"k tett e export figures, a London contemporary lee '°'ne to the conclusion that "we gave nearly ele,P,et cent more trade to foreigners than we did th 'lves obtain from them." This is nonsense; e hulk of our trade being barter there 6114 be somewhere an equivalent on our side t all trade we "give" to the foreigners. there is. It is strange thatevith each recur- `mg annual return this balance of trade scarecrow lhould have to be explained afresh to the timid minds which it is used to frighten. The bulk of It is accounted for by what are called "invisible `,,nsts," or the official returns are by no means "'replete, and by the earnings of our world-wide eu-rfr L.,Ying trade, while the import values also cover ineigat and other charges which are not included acrtile eXport figures. The balance left after all uatments are made, represents the actual J "'e retion of wealth to the country. If our foreign t 'rade if 3. profitable, the profit will show itself in :urger value ceming into the country than goes ult. If a merchant does not receive in value more an he gives,he is doing an unremunerative trade, ted will soon have to seek the protection of the ',aLtrruptcy Court. The same rules apply to rernational trade carried on upon a barter basis. any case, every cargo imported gives employ- to, British industry in handling, and dia- lributing, while a great part of our imports is raw 414terial for our industries. We are assuredly not hying upon our national capital, as increasing in- t't)i) Me tax assessments show,but growing wealthier °en' foreign trade. And British capital is not eltnited in its use by the coast line of the British lands, but gives employment, even to British I:hour, in all parts of the world. The figures for the Past year show how elastic British trade can mos even under exceptional drawbacks; and it may not at the moment be increasing 'QaPs and bounds, we may look forward confi- inlY to another period of expansion later on.</p>
        <p>Writer of the "Celebrities at Home " series, a generally one of the most interesting of Ai in the Wortit,tells this week in an account egh„r Stephen Phillips chez lui the story of the a "re Rill • g host It is a singular narrative,and PPea tho, r 3 to be as Judy authenticated as any of lies"B which the Society for Psychical Research Thed, After his literary successes and his to the stage, the author of. " Christ in sew," took a house on Egharn Hill, near the hy irated Runnymede Woods, where, doubtless, Th,„°Peal to woo the Muses to some good purpose. art; gilost, however, intervened,f or hardly had the hot;arits entered the house than one and all gave tic alleging alleging as the ground of their dissatisfac- otC,the wierd noises which were heard through house, and which they lik ,ned to the " attendant on the strangulation of a child." terri`g9illiPa, being a man of sense, harangued his hia-:'"ad household on the folly of their fears, but • IMPassioued rhetoric produced no result. The aocaortv4ot problem" in the poet's menage took a very tit,: Phase, and in a day or two all tia3 fled. The • gr'es that Mr Phillips invited a friend to stay t 1 1411 t in the house, and that subsequElutly, al- Lgu he had given no intimation of the particu- illsof nueuiet visitor which haunted the place eal Lr, lend told him that at a seance of the Psychi- laerateiefl he was informed that a child had been pric.red in the neighbourhood, and that the cul- Thiarevisited the scene and re-acted the tragedy. thy ttleems the weakest part of the narrative, as O se.BYehical Society carries out its investigation Whioitific lines, and does not hold eeauces in !pie, the mernbers get in cominunication with ,4„11;a• departed or otherwise. The ghost, however eo .418 t real enough and potent enough to throw 41either r Phillips' hands a house which he can occupy nor sub-let.</p>
        <p>A li 1:43,54 ew scheme for training domestic servants has Drtia started by some ladies in Cheshire. We may appilibrne) however, that the same difficulty will s 4 419 in the cage of the here. The young girl</p>
        <p>of humble parentage who intends to enter into domestic service has no inclination to thoroughly prepare herself for the work when she can, by taking an inferior situation, make money immedi ately. This ordinary position as a general in a- numerous and poor family, or as a kitchen- maid, is often irksome, not so much because the duties are necessarily so as because the mistress is often lacking in tact and con- sideration, and is irritable. The girl longs for the freedom of the factory hand and the worthless compliments from the other sex which are extended to the shop-girl. She therefore drifts into one or other of these ocdupations ; the pay is poor, and when she has to board herself she is not so well fed. Probably when mistresses be- come a little more considerate to their servants, and the latter realize how hard iq the independent life, the growing generation will look more friendly on domestic work, and be prepared to take pains to obtain skill in it. It is a poor compliment to English housewifery to suggest there is no art or learning to be acquirwl for the proper manage. ment of a house. lu Germany they think differently, and if they do not give the servants a special education, at least the mistresses have one.</p>
        <p>Football, for the time being, takes absolute pre- eminence in Warrington. Th;s week-end the local Rugby team has to do battle in the final ties of the Northern Union and the South-West Lancs. Cup Competitions. As it is fifteen years since Warrington won a cup at football or even appeared in the final tie, the individual who looks upon the game as an invention of the Evil One and its devo- tees as maniacs—even he will be able to realise something of the interest and excitement occasioned locally by the proud position at present occupied by the Warrington team. Unquestionably the people are football mad, and there will be , some marvelling by the phlegmatic stay-at-homes ' this Saturday when there is an exodus of about 5,000 males, all bent on accompanying the War- rington players to Leeds. Ministers might well decry too close an application to sport on the part of the public, for it is certain that few of the 5,000 will be inclined for attendance at church on the day following the excitements of a cup-final, and some no doubt, will be incapable. As a rule a football crowd is good-tempered and well-behaved, with a noticeable predilection to " Much of that 'liveliness" will be apparent in Warrington on Saturday evening, should the local team brim-, home the coveted Northern Union Cup, and who will say that the rejoicings are ill-timed or uncalled for? Our national gaines take up a great deal of the people's time but they exert a beneficial influence on the national character by fostering manliness and a love of outdoor exercise and physical attainment. We sincerely hope that success will crown tke efforts of the Warrington team in both finals, and that they will bring unexpected honour to the town in company with two cups. Their association friends have already annexed one trophy and the example ,nust be followed,</p>
        <p>Mr ttehtand his crusaders will soon alienate from themselves all the sympathy they have won in their avowed efforts to wards purifying the Anglican ceremonial, if they are the cause of further un- seemly disturbances such as disgraced the pro- ceedings at St Mary-le-Bow ',last week, when Dr Winnington Ingrain was confirmed as Bishop of London. Instead of being the solemn, signifi- cant ceremonial it should have been, th,.; confirlflm. tion service resolved itself into an inaudible pro- nunciation of ritual and a very audible aerie b of disorderly cries, objections, and interruptions. We have before expressed our admiration of the Bishop's personal character, and we are glad to see that his dignity rose superior to the unpleaa- ant surroundings. lie himself emerged trium- phant from the fray and was accorded an ovation outside the Courch which proved that the pro- ceedings of the Kensitites had defeated their own object, for anything in the nature of legitimate protest was forgotten in the general desire to sup- port the chief sufferer. There is plenty of scopii for action by those who desire to see the State Church freed from Rotnish practices, without any vulgar resort to violence, and we trust anything of the kind will be openly discountenanced by every- body. Mr Kensit seems to think he was the hero of the occasion. It would perhaps be of some service to him could he know that in the estimation of ninety-nine people in every hundred he was the "villain of the piece" and perhaps, the fool too.</p>
        <p>A movement is on foot to revive the May-day procession of draught horses and tradesmen's turn- outs in Warrington. There was a time when this was a popular institution in the town but within recent years it has fallen into disuetude. Wlr- rington has perhaps more horses and vehicles than , any manufacturing town of a similar size in the kingdom and the competition which an organised I procession engenders creates a spirit of emulation amongst the carters and has a tendency to induce them to take a greater pride in their work. The success of such a procession of gaily decorated horses largely depends upon favourable weather. Within recent years, wet weather has been fre- quently experienced Gn May days, and the be- draggled appearance of the horses has spoiled the show. It is to be hoped that the organisers of the Warrington procession will be well favoured in re- gard to weather. The ,date will probably be Saturday, May 4th.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-04-26_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>MATTERS OF MOMENT. A Provincial Letter.</head>
        <p>MATTERS OF MOMENT. A Provincial Letter.</p>
        <p>Never since the day Mr Lowe proposed to place a tax on matches, has there been se much commo- tion raised by any Budget proposals as those that have been agitating bhe coun ry, from South Wales to Newcastle-on-Tyne, during the pest week. t-ir Michael Hicks-Beach is credited with being not over-sensitive to criticism, and it is well for him if it be true that he is not He is, at any rate, an old Parliamentary hand, and does not appPar to take hi:l critics too seriously. When told tha the nati could ill afford to pay an additi .ital twopence in the shape of income-tax, that the export coal tax of a shilling pe- ton will diminish profits and may adversely affect wages, and that the extra half- penny per pound on sugar wll be felt by every I man, woman, and child in the kingdom, the I.Autn- cellor of the Exchequer coolly answers that all these things were in his mind before, and that he hopes the past year's experience will turn the thoughts of the people to the in!erests of economy. • ir William Harcourt,who had ben in Sir Michael's place, said the same, and approved both o the proposal to add to the ineome-tax and of t reasons given for doing it. The House of Co mons seemed non, lussed, and approved likewise of this particular Budget proposal. But of the whole three additions to taxation set forth in the Budget the country has not heard the last There is a great outc,y as yet a ;ainst imposing an export duty upon ' coal, notwithstanding that the Chancellor has ex- pressed his willingness to exempt existing contracts from the operation of his proposal. Meanwhile the proposal that will Effect-, the whole community has created the least complaint, or at any rate has been least heard of from the critics, and possi ly for the very reason that the sugar duty touches every body, and notindividual classes only, together with a knowledge that the war, whether approved or not ,• has now to be paid for, has caused the least 1 grumbling.</p>
        <p>"Gallant little Wales," to employ a Gladstonism is naturally very jubilant over the prospect of a I R.,yal residence being established in the Princi- pality. It is only a repo. tas yet that gives rise to the jubilation, but it has been several days " in the air" without eliciting contradiction, and there are other reasons for accepting it as correct. It is to the effect that King Edward has purchased the charming and historic residence .of Glynllifou,where Princes Victoria of Wales stayed for some days a short time ago. Glynllifou is described as one of the stately homes of England," having a history reaching back to very remote times in the annals of the district. The particular locality, commanding as it does extensive views of mountains and the sea, is considered as leaving nothing to be desired, and the purchase is, of coune,regarded as another proof of King Edward's appreciation of the beautiful in ,</p>
        <p>tho census return Al come dribbling in from Tarim parts of the provinces, they tend to show</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-04-26_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
