      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-04-26_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>EXAMINER, FPIPAY, APRIL 26, 1901</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FPIPAY, APRIL 26, 1901</p>
        <p>that the driftin2 of the population from village to town, and from rural to urban districts has been going on with undiminished force, and that in some of the countiev, such as those of Fluntingdon, Kent, and S imerser, the rate of exodus is so great as to have emphasized anew the need for so re-modelling the land-laws as to bring the people into closer attachment with the soil. It is curious to observe in i his connecion that journals representing both parties in the State appear to be agreed upon this point. However, much has been done to improve the condition of the poorer class ts in the agricul- tural districts, it is admitted that muck remains yet to be done. Elementary education, the provision of allotments, better dwellings, and a general rais- ing of the standard of comfort, are making ihe lot of the rural labourer and artisan more endurable, and for any greater advancement yet to be accom- plished it is felt that rural residents must to a large extent look to themselves. While waiting for Par- liament to enact better laws in regard to land hold- ing they can, through the parish, district, and 'county councils achieve many of the reforms that are very desirable. It rests with the electors, of course, to put progressive men as their representa- tives on these bodies, and if they do this they may under existing laws advance their condition materi- ally. The mere rate saver, however, whose one idea is to keep 'down the rates, should be avoided. As a rule he is a hindrance, and sometimes a positive danger, saving the rates at the cost of health or of life itself. If half the energy that is wasted in agitation and crying for further amendment of the laws were devoted to the application of reforms that have already been achieved an immense im- provement in the general agricultural conditions would be effected.</p>
        <p>Many have been the anxious inquiries for some days past as to what was known of the prospects of the Sale of Liquor to Children Bill. Consequent. upon these inquires a question appears to have been put down in the House of Commons, but only to disappear mysteriously and without any ex- planation. Pie House has been moved from con- stituencies in the provinces to take especial interest In tkis question, with the result that Mr Balfour has at last been induced to reply to the effect that the Government "fully appreciate the public Interest in the Bill," but that at the present stage it was not convenient to indicate what private memtiers' bills they would be prepared specially to facilitate. It appeared that a memorial had been signed by 285 menbers, asking special facili- ties for the Bill, which, if not granted, will inevitably prevent its becoming law this session But the interest increases and there is still room for hope.</p>
        <p>St Geotge's day can scarcely be said to have ' fairly "caught on "in the provinces hitherto. Its observance on Tuesday was not necessarily such as to discourage promoters of the movement, and if the Mayors and other persons in authority only do as certain of them have this year, in the way of ask- ing the public to " hang out their barmen OU the outer walls" on that -day, the revival or the deve; loptnent of the custom. may sti'l be expected to follow,lßut there is something incongruous in the idea of patriotic Englishmen importing continental blooms in honour of their patron saint. For St. Patrick's Day the shamrock comes from Ireland, and the nineteenth of April generally synchronises with an abundant supply of primroses in the Eng- lish m adows and woodlands. If the festival of St. Ge n.ge should really become one of our reeog- nised flower days, it certainly might be expected to lend a stimulus to the cultivation of the earlier varieties ot the rose, and in this way perhaps justify the observance.</p>
        <p>Tho3e who are apprehensive as to the po-sible dangers of electric tramways are reminded by the serious occident at Northa.npton the ooer day that other systems hive their own special risks. In the centre of the town i he horses attached to a tramcar tonic fright and bolted. The driver did all he could to avert an accident; but unfortunately the car was heavily loaded inside and out, and the vad on an incline. Against the mrimentum of the heavy car, added to the pace of the horseikthe brake was powerless, and at a sharp curve in the road the brake-chain parted and the car crashed over on its side. Eighteen persons were more or less severely injured, but under the circumstances there was cause for congratulation that things were no worse. It is a kind of matter, however, that demands serious inquiry. The public have a right to require safety, and life ought not to he endan- gered by the nuglect of any precaution that can possibly be taken. At the same time, the accident in this case should serve, tta already suggested, to remind a certain class of persons, that be the faults of the electric tramway, what they may, bolting is not one of them.</p>
        <p>Returns issued this week show that recruiting for the year resulted in the a44ition of 49,260 men to the regular service, and 37,853 t ) the militia. When it was a, its height in the provinces a few months since, the general feeling appeared to be that recruiting vas likely to produce wonders. But as a matter of fact, the report just issued does not indicate that there bas been any advance to- wards a solution of the problems so perplexing to those persons who appear to see the shadow of "conscription" ever looming on the horizon, lf the emergency recruits raised during the year be set aside, it will be found that the supply of men for military purposes is considerably short of the demand, and this too, under the impetus given by the wive of patriotic fervour thit recently swept over the whole country. The chief remedies that suggest thAniselves are those of better pay and in- creased Concessions, but the taxpayer is scarcely in a mood to willingly bear of anything more in this direction zit present.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-04-26_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>RUNCORN NOTES. [BY A CORRESPONDENT.]</head>
        <p>RUNCORN NOTES. [BY A CORRESPONDENT.]</p>
        <p>The annual meetings of the Runcorn Board of Guardians. and Rural District Councd were bald on Tuesday last. The Board of Guardians decided on a change in its chairraln, and elevated Mr Joseph Stokes from the vice chair, and conferred the vies- chairmanship upon Mr A R Norman(Roneorn). This was an arrangement, too, with whirl' Mr J Moseley Williams (the retiring chairman) was said to have I expressed satisfaction.</p>
        <p>The Rural District Councillors. were, however divided into two sections, one being in Favour oft a change, and the other in Favour of the old order of things. Mr :Samuel Woodward. was mentioned as the chairman, and would undoubtedly have got the position had he not so courteously declined t enter the arena against his Friend, Mr Joseph Knowles. The withdrawal of Mr Woodward thug restorei the harmony of the gath3ring, ard Mr J Knowles was elected chairman for the fourth year, with Mr s Woodward as his deputy.</p>
        <p>The Runcorn Rural District Council are still troubled with the Grapperthall Tannery nuisance, ;so wrongly located at Thelwall, and have great difficulty in getting to the bottom of the question. Now, it. has teen decided to call in the services of ! Dr Penny to report on the " Invf•tsry " ; and. 1 trust, the result will be the el;mination oF the " nuisance," or its identification in either the !" ah tract" or " concrete "—Whilst I am dealing with smells, I should like to call the attention of the Runcorn Urban District Council to the very bad smell which asmils people .$31,8 they come around what iA known as Eavage7s Corner, opposite the Bellwood Buildings. Some days it is abominable, and now tho hot weather is here it's getting worse.</p>
        <p>On Thursday afternoon the cunr-side and hanks of the Manchester Ship Cant were lined for miles with peonle determinPd on getting a sight of the torpedo flotilla which is paying a ceremonial visit to Manchester. The boats, which included the " Leven," " wolf," " Leopard," " Locust." " Bat," " Panther,'" Fairy," and "Gipsy" passed the Run- corn layby at about 3 o'clock, steaming about 94 miles an hour, and presented a strileng spectacle. Th.i rear was taken up with the Manchester Ship Canal Company's tug " Bastham," on the decks of which were the Canal officials and a cinematograph busily at work. The ships in the docks flew their distinctive colours, and flags were " dipped "at all the principal points.</p>
        <p>The Runcorn Volunteers are expected to return to their homed tit Monday, and the latett intelligence is to the eff ,ct that the Transport Tagus left St. Vincent, on Friday morning. The exact time of the Ru, c•lrn Volunteers' advent cannot, of course, be arrived At, hut I understand that telegrams will be forwarded from Southampton and Chester giving e.e exact time, and that our townsman Mr Mack, of Dolph Bridge,will, as usuaLdinburse the informa- tion as soon as it arrives.</p>
        <p>_ HARRIS'S WILTSHIRE RAOIN. Gold MOM, Paris 1900.- 10EN A/IMM Za. Bold•street, Warrington, —Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-04-26_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>RUNCORN RURAL. DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL. DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>Annual Meeting. " The The!wall Mystery."</p>
        <p>The annual awe ingot ,ns Runitorn Rural District Council was held at the Workhouse,Dutton, on Tuesday. •re were pres-nt : Messrs J Koowles, S Woodward, C E Linaker, T Wright, J Stoke.,A El taloa% J Mclntyre. Admiral J P Jones-Parrs , Mysirs T Darlington, 8 Houghton, R Bil,ingtou, A Eaton, W H Bradford, T Lewis. C Ul.ke, J Edwards, J Taylor, E Wallington, C Brethsrton, T Antwiss, P Gli ave, J Whitlow, J B 1 haw, El Mawdsloy, A G Gusst, J MmnlislJ, J Spruce, George Pilling. T Baxter with Messrs George F Ashton (clerk), W E Hough (assistant clerk), .1 Farrington (in vector) D ggle and Swinton (sursieyors),and Dr Adams (medical officer).</p>
        <p>THE CHAIRMANSHIP Mr T Darlington proposed Lost Mr Joseph Knowles, whn was well known to them all, be again elected chairman. Mr S Horton seconded. Mr A 11 ralbot proposed as an amendment that Mr S. Woodward be their chairman. Ele did so without any personal feeling against Mr Kbowles, and because he felt that the honour should go round. Hiving paid a high tribute to Mr Woodward's capabilities, he gave pl.ce to Mr Clarke, who seconded the amendment, endorsing all that was said. _ _</p>
        <p>Admiral Jones pointed ou: the great mistake of changing the chairman, and contended they were only justified in changing until they got a good one, where- upon it should be the blast of the Council that they could keep him. (Laughter.) Mr Darlington in defence of his proposal argued- that as the chairmanship carried a seat with it on the County Bench, it was only fair that the farmers of the district should secure to themselves the opportunity of patting a tenant farmer th.reon—(Elear, hear)--to look after their interests. (Applause.) Mr Woodward with the permission of his proposer and seconder, withdrew from the contest, and Mr Knowles' re.election was the signal of loud applause. Mr Kocwies acknowledged the honour. THE VICE-CHAIRMAN Mr Talbot proposed that the honour be conferred upon Mr Woodward. _</p>
        <p>Mr Antwiss sscond d and the motion was received with applause. Mr Woodward acknowledged the honour. TUE PORT SANITARY AUTHORITY. Mr John Ll'hiby, asaistaut secretary to the Local Government Board forwarded printed notice of the in- quiry they had instrueted one of their inspectors to hold at Manchester on the 23rd inst. The Clerk intimated that the members told off to act in the matter were attending the ir</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND NORTHWICH LIGHT RAILWAYS. letter was read from the Light Railway Cimmis- sioners informing the District Council that they would on Satirday th- 4t,li of May at 9.45 a.m. open a public inquiry at the Drill Hall Northavich, as to the expedi- ency of granting the application which has been m de to them for an Order to authorise the above named light railways, The Clerk said tne Council would recollect , When the matter was before them On a pre- vious occasion notice came that the promoters of the schema intended to amend their draft, and it was decided to await the amended pro- posals. This came a little over • fortnight ago, and It was then thought that the inqiiry could not he held on short notico. The result was that the committee deputed to deal with the matter hid not since met. 11-presentatives of the County Council had met the pro- moters hut had not yet come to terms, and he (the clerki had written to Mr Potts (Clerk to the County Council) for a copy of what had been suggested. Mr Potts. however, replied that he had forwarded them to the promoters, but had not lunit a copy. fts. however, hoped to have them back in a day or two, and he would let the Council have a copy. On the proposition of Mr Woodward it Wee cleci led that the c erk be empowered to call a special meeting of the New Parliamentary Committee, and that they he empowered to appoint delegates to attend the inquiry. It was subsequently decided tbat the names of the re- presentazives of the several districts through which the proposed railway piss-td be aided to ttis committee.</p>
        <p>RAILWAY AND CANAL BRIDWRL The Carlisle Rural Dis•rict o.3uncil forwarded a copy of a circular which they have sent t 3 each Rural Distrct Council in the Kingdom asking if they will combine for the purpose of procuring an Act of . Parliament to be passed to enable District C )uncils to comnel Railway and Canal Companies to rn Ike their bridges strong enough to carry steam rollers and other ordinary traffic of the present day. Much sympathy was expressed with the Carlisle Con Icil,.and on the motion of Mr T Baxter, seconded by Mr J Stokes it was decided to support the resolulon, and to petition the local M.P.'s.</p>
        <p>THE THELWALL MYSTERY. DETERMINED ON A SOLUTION. The Clerk said in AC:I gdance with the requirements of the last meeting he had been in communication with Mr Codling, sanitary surveyor of Blanchester, and Mr Alfred Smetharn, F.C.S., F.I.C. of Livirpool. and bo'h gentlemen were prepared to undertake the work of in. cti ng the Tannery, and the fees mentioned were £5 59 and expenses, and £7 7s, with additional fees for any analytical work. ' Mr S Woctd ward moved that an exput be appaintel to rnske a thorough inspection of the Thel wall Tannery and/ the alleged nuisance root and branch. He felt they were obliged to take the step in justice to themselves as well as to the public. lie advocated strongly they should appoint a man who combined the qualifisations of s sanitary expert, an analytical chemist, and who was a scientist lie did not care whether the 3ost was £7—£l7 or £7O. so long as the result was satisfactory. Mr Baxter sec 'laded. Mr Woodward mentioned the name of Sir Henry Roscoe. Mr Wallington proposed that Mr Alfred Smethans be engaged to make the investigation. He believed him to be a most competent man, and be was one who would not be biassed one way or the other. Mr r. Wright said Mr Stnetham analysed some samples of water from Halton, and they were perfectly satisfied that hews a very. clever man. _.</p>
        <p>Mr Clarke seconded Mr Wallingtgn's propoeition.</p>
        <p>The Inspector sail what they wantei was an expert tanner, a gentleman who could tell whether there had peen any diff-rence in the process of tanning which would give nff the smell complained of. De Adams, at this stage, said he had the results of the examination of certain waters from Thalwall,and hp suggested that he might read them. The result of the bacteriological examination showed one sample con- tained 1.476 organiams per centimetre. Thii number the analyst said, was auffieiently high to condemn the water for drinking purposes. The samples of water contained organisms belonging to the diptherioid group He (Dr Adams) considered that the three waters which were used for drinking purposes by Mr Quenk Mr IVickers, and Mr Reynolds were unfit for driAing pur- poses without previous boiling and Alt-ring. An ' improved water Supplg was required. It seemed to him that the cause of all the diphtheria that they had had in Thel wall might he summed up under the bead of sanit- ation and impute and defeeive watn. supplies. Mr Wallington quite agreed with the doctor on the' question of the defective water supplies, but his point was that people ware aeizel all at once with a faintiug feeling, and it was to find out the cause of this that he ormoled Mr Smetham should make an examination of I the place. Dr Adams said be hoped it was not supposed for one . . .</p>
        <p>moment that he opposed an expert. He was only too glad to have any number of experts. He had other watere in Thelwall in connection with this outbreak that he wreald very much like to have examined. Mr Tdbot proposed that Dr Penny be appointed to make the investigation This was seconded, and on a vote being taken Dr Penny was appointed by twelya votes to eleven. The Clerk said he had a hatch of letters complaining of the Thelwall nuis.nce. They were from k Ditonfi ld 9 Leigh Villas, Grapnenhall ; L Hmash•li, 26, Salisbury- street-W arringtont; A Aspey,Thelwali Brook, Lvmm ; D Morris 154, Wiliarsnonl-r lad, Warrington ; L Laach, The Stables, Cuerden Hall, Thelwall ; and John Vickers, Temperance House, Thel wall. W %Fri ngton It was proposed and seconded that the letters lie on the table. The Clerk said he had another letter from Dr Id sAr•hton the medical nifieer of health to the Warrington Rural District Council. Efe did not know whether Ve nuisance or s supposed ro AffAnt the Warrington Rural District Council. and if Dr Sophton's report was given in a ritivlte capacity perhaps the Council wo.uld take no no.ir.e of it. 111' Wendward eid not think Dr Seplaton ought to come into their district. Mr Maur:field said the district they were now speak- ing orformerly belonged to the VVerrirenn D•Crict Council, and Dr Saphton was the medical officer for it Mr Linaker said in Dr Svhton's opinion there was nothing delec ive in the sewers. He did not think the le'ter was private. Fief took it that Mr Naylor was simply defending himself aYainat the insinuation made in that room that his promisee were insanitary. It was at ,engrat deoided no to read the letters. THE ISOLATION HOSPITAL QUEITION Mr Woodward, in accnrdanoe with notic• given at the last matting moved that the chairm*n. the vice-chair mall. Messrs Chadwick, Moseley Williams, Linaker. Bar/it:term, Wright, Davenport and Talbot be appointed a cmumittee to deal with the question of an isolation hnlnital fer the district: at an early date. This was seconded and carried. THE SEIVERAOS CIIHIRTION AT HATTON. The cloor rep,toi that :he c munittoe whn were SP/Pointed to consider the Hetton Sewerage q lestion met tn^ agent and sub.:agent of Sir Gilbert Greenall, and aft" discussing the mstter with them, Mr White Pro- mised to write easing what Sir Gilbert would be willing to do. He had reeeivel the following letter that (moms. der) morning :—Moore Hall, Warrington, April 223 d, 1901. Hatton Sewage : saw Sir Gilbert I Greenall today after mating your committee, and he is</p>
        <p>desirous of doing all he can to assist you in regard to the matter. The estimated cost of dealing with the sewage by land filtration at the place where we met is stated to be £lOO, without rent of land and maintenance. If your committee will agree to pay £25 a year for four years, we will deal with the sewage on its arrival at the Waco referred to. At the end of four years, if the ex- periment is success: ul, the arrangesnent can be con- tinued upon such terms as shall be eventually agreed upon. If unsuccessful, or if it turns out to be a nuisance, some other plan will have to be adopted. Of course a proper agreement must be entered into. Yours truly, John Weits, G F Ashton, Esq. Mr Mawdsley moved that the offer be accepted, and that the Corporation ef Warrington be written to with a view to their terms being ac.lept( d. Mr Eoliths w sscooded, and the motion was carried. AN OBSTRUCTION AT MOORE.</p>
        <p>Mr Weave revirted dbat toe Committee who had con- sidered the footpath obstruction question found that it was purely an obstruction with regard to the (railway company. There bad been no deviation whatever since the hurdles were put up. The hurdles were put up by the Ship Canal Company, but before the work was com- pleted it was tralssferred to the London and North- Western Railway Company. The Company had put proper steps to the first foJtpath,but they had neglected the second footpath. It was at length decided on the motion of Mr Gleave to request the London and North Western Railway Company to complete their work.</p>
        <p>THE HEALTH OF THE DISTRICT,</p>
        <p>Dr Adams reported that there had bee.o nue case of scarlet fever at Latchford, another at The!wall. and an obscure case of diphtheria at Toelurid!. He had visited the premises and directed the inspector to investigate the water supplies to these and other houses in the neighbourhood.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-04-26_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>STREET ACCIDENT AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>On Saturday night, a woman named Caroline I Dlollison, aged 66 year, 12, Arthur-street,was walk- ing along Widnes-road when she was knocked down and run over by a bonne and cart. Her ribs were fractured, and she was removed to the Accid- ent Hospital and attended by Dr dwards. NEW POOR RATES. _ _ .</p>
        <p>—„ On Saturday, Me-srs G L Davies an I Imison confirmed a new poor rare for As.on-by-Satton. of 11 44 in the X, to realise £299 us 21; and a similar rate for Aston Grange, of Is 41 in the £, to realise £3l 18s 84; and a poor rate for Su, ton, of is 24 in the X, to realise £368 7s 81 A RUNCORN PRIZE DOG.</p>
        <p>At the Blickburu Dog Sho v, on Saturday, Mrs T Jones, Leinster-gardens, Runcorn, wa3 successful in carrying off no fewer than five prizes with her bo z3i, aged nine months. Tae dog was awarded firQt prize ia the puppy class, first in the novice class, two special prizes for the bet novice in the show, and a special prize for the best puppy in the thow.</p>
        <p>ntINCORN NiAGISTRATRB SWORN IN. At the Runcorn Petty Sessions, on Monday, be- fore Mr F J Norman, Mr A R Norman took the oath of allegiance to King Edward VII., and was re- sworn as a justice of the peace tor the county. Mr RII Posnett qualified as a justice of the peace by virtue of his position as chstrman of the Runcorn Urban District Council, and also took the oath of allegiance.</p>
        <p>_ CHESTER. CoNSrSTORY COURT. At Chester Consistory Court yesterday, Canon Maitland Wood, vicar of Runcorn, was authorised to remove seats and screens in order to make room for an organ, to erect a new organ at a cost of about £650, and to place a stained-glass window in the church as a memorial to the late Mrs 13 Mark- ham, a Sunday school teacher and a district visitor in the parish. ' Sr. PAUL'S CHURCH SUNDAY SCIIOoL, WIDNES The teachers at St Paul's Church Sunday Soho°. held their quarterly social in the Parochial Rooml on Thursday last. About fifty teachers partook os tea, which had been prepared by Mesdames Stocksf Ma'am, Carter. and the Misses Carter, Wright, and, Mr Barker. Games were subsequently indulged in During the evening Mr J Hough gave a reading, and Mr D J Jones rendered several sons, REMARKABLE SEIZURE OF A RUNCORN WORKMAN. On Monday morning, at 11 o'clock, 'Norms, Lswe, Malcolm-street, Runcarn, a labourer working at Matheson's Works, Widnes, was seized with a paralytic stroke while following his employment as a burner. All the use was taken out of his right side, and the sight of his right aye was dangerously impaired. Re was conveyed home by four of his workmates in a chair, which was sp-cially impro- vised for the occasion. He is making satisfactory progress and a few days rest will probably see him restored to perfect health. ARCHDEACON BARBER AT RUNCoRN. On Tuesday afternoon Archdeacon Barber of Chester held his aanual visitation for the admission of churchwardens to their duties: Usually the cere- mony has been held at Frodsham, on this occasion it took place at Runcorn Parish Church. The formal business was preceded by a short service, conducted by the Rev Canon Maitlalld Wood, vicar, assisted by the Rev H B Firtn and the choir. The Archdeacon, in the course of his " charge " referred to the change of the venue of the visitation and said in acquiescence with the expressed wish of 'several of the wardens last year that he should hold the visitation at Runcorn he had done so.— The churchwardens and sidesmen pres.nt from the varioneparts of the rural deanery of Frodsham then signed the deelara.ion. A FIRE PROMPTLY DEALT WITH. A fire broke out in the cottage hsuse belonging to William Smith, 94, Cooper-street, Simms Cross, Widnes, on Monday. The fire originated under- neath the stairs among sOille Old 0100139. An alarm was immediately raised and P.O Si Thornton and Dawson arriving on the scene common cared with the fire station. The fire brigade, under the com- mand of the deputy superintendent, made for the spot with all speed but by their arrival the fire bad already been extinguished and their services were not required. Beyond the burning of clothes and some of the woodwork little damage has been sus- tained, though serious results might have ensued if the fire had not been discovered in its early stages. WORKS GUTTED NEAR WIDNES. Shortly before ten o'clock on Monday night fire broke out at Farnworth Pith and Size Company's works, Widnea, and, burning fiercely, extended rapidly to the whole of one spacious bailing used as a storeroom. The fire brigade arrived on the acme, and fortunately succeeded in preventing the flames extending to the manufacturing part of the works and to the General Electrolytic Company's works, in the adjacent buildings. The building where the fire originated was gutted, and damage done amounting to between £2,000 and £3,000. T 43 property belongs to the executors of the late Joseph Bushby, Rainhill, and is insured. The origin of the outbreak is unknown. WIDNES BOYS AND THE Blum. At the Widnes Police Court, on Monday, before the Mayor (Alderman G I Neil) and Alderman S Sadler, three boys, -James Kennedy, Matthew Burns, Warrington-road, and James Fitzpatrick, Lea-street,were charged with Et 3alinß 23 fire bricks, valu i le Ild, the property of she Lancashire Metal Works, on Saturday aft rnoon,—P.C. More said that at 230 on Saturday afternoon he wa4 on duty at the Lancashire Metal Works when be saw the three prisoners taking the bricks outside the works by putting their bands through a window composed of iron bars. They afterwards put them in a me al wag- gnu. When it was filled they walked away, and he . walked round to intercept them. When aoprehen- ided and charged the prisoners replied, " Yes; we took them."—As there wa4 nothing known against ithe prisoners, their parents were bound over for three months in sureties of .£5 for their future good behaviour.</p>
        <p>CRAWFORD S</p>
        <p>CREAM</p>
        <p>CRACKER,S,I CRISP AND CREAAY</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-04-26_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>RUNCORN FREE LIBRARY COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN FREE LIBRARY COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>Annual Meeting.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the above Committee was held at the Council Chamber, at tee Town Hall, on Tuesday when there were present :—lflrssrs 9 Pope, W Handley, D Bisbrown. M J Sweet man, W Hankinson, F Hutton, T Riley, ARN rman, E Imisnn, JEI Kirkham with Mr E Marshall (secretary of ttie District Council), who acted as clerk.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-04-26_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>ELECTION OF CHAIRMAN.</head>
        <p>ELECTION OF CHAIRMAN.</p>
        <p>Mr T Handley proposed that Mr Pope be re.e'ected chairman, ferliog sure they could not do better. He had been in offi-e one year and had made them a very good chairman and ho felt that in the year to come Mr Pope would even do batter. Mr W Hankinson seconded for he was fully convinced that what Mr Handley said would come to pass, and besides Mr Pope ;cm not only a competent Chairman but a genial and kindly one. Mr D Biabrown cordially supported the proposition. The resolution was then put and Mr. Pops was unanimously re-eleoted. Mr. Pope acknowledged the honour.</p>
        <p>M J. Sweetman then prop )sxl that Mr I) Bisbrown be re-ele :ted Vice- airman. and although Mr Bisbrown was in favour of a change Mr. Imison seconded and the motion was agreed to.</p>
        <p>THE LATE LIM/ARIAN.</p>
        <p>Mr D. Bisbrown then intimated he had a melanchily duty to perform. They were all aware they had lost their librarian, Mr J D Jones, by death, and they met that night under • sharp and common sorrow, for he was sure every member of that committee held the late Mr Jones in high esteem. His death had created • lois which would be sensibly felt for a long time consequently hi wished to move a resolution showing their appreciation ' of Mr Jones' services, and also a vote of condolence with his widow in her sad bereavement. There were some Men of whom it °quid be said that the more you had ';(i do with them the more attached you became to them— Ouch a man was Mr J D Jones. (Hear, hear). Lie did not think there was any gentleman in the town who had a closer acqaaintance with Mr Jones than himself. For 36 years he bad been a psrsonal friend of his. They were brought up together as lads, and were firm and fast friends up to the time of his death ; and during the whole of that time he never know Mr. Jones to do a shabby or shady thing. (Hear. bear.) He was truly honest and upright in everything; be was straightfor- ward to a fault ; and the transparency of his character was to be admired. (Hear, hear.) He proposed " that the members of the Runcorn Free Library Committee acknowledge the loss the Committee have sustained by the d, ath of Mr J D Jones, the librarian, and wish to place on record their appreciation of his services, and [ expense their deep sympathy with his widow in her sad ' trreavement." Mr A R Norman seconded. Mr Jones was indeed a Man for whom hey all had a great respect. The most striking thing was the way in which Mr Jones had woiked himself up from a lowly position and he felt a character like that deserved every credit and com- manded their highest esteem. (Hear, hear.) Mr W Handley who felt Mr Jones' death very keenly paid a high tribute to the work of Mr Jones. Two of them— Mr T Riley and himself— had been me nbers of the Library Committee since its formation and they could testify to the devotion of Mr Jones to :his duties as librarian. Not only had he educated the people of Runcorn in the workings of the Libraries act, but before that he took a great interest in the literary life of Runaore. It was very largely due to his devotedness that they owed their library of to-day. (Hear, hear). Thanks to Mr Jones' efforts their library would compare with any in any town of a like Biz and indeed, in one particular he ventured to assert an other library in Cheshire could compare with it, and that was in the collection of books it contained relative to the County and particular dis- trict. All that was due to Mr Jones. He was unique They hadn't to look round or advertise when they wanted a librarian ; they found a man ready to their hands. He only hoped that the work that Mr Jones had been so largely instrumental in Freeing, would be ' carried ea as well in the future. (Hear, hear.) The Chairman felt that Mr Jones was a man of whom it could be said" What his hand found to do, be did with all his might," and one who had made the Public Library a benefit to Runcorn. (Hear, hear). The resolution was then passed, the Members stand- ing.</p>
        <p>OTHER BUSINESS.</p>
        <p>On the motion of Mr W Hankioson, seconded by Mr Sweermathit waa decided in consequence of the death of the librarian the deputy librarian(Mre J D Jonee)be paid at the rate of £5 per month pending the appointment of a librarian.— Acclunts were passed for payment amounting to £5 9.—lt was decided to discontinue the services of the csreaker.—Messrs W Handley, Cunning- ham Hutton and the Chairman and Vice-chairman were elected a committee to draw up certain recommenda- tions to be submitted to the neat ordinary meeting of the library controlling bolo</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-04-26_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>CAMDEN WESLEYAN CHAPEL RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>CAMDEN WESLEYAN CHAPEL RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Sunday School Anniversary.</p>
        <p>St. Paul's Church, Runcarn, was filled with large congregations on Sunday and Monday last, the occasion of the anniversary services in connection with the Camden Ch4pel, Lowlands-road. The Rev 3 H Goodman wag the preacher, and in the morning he preached from the text "And they brought young children to Him that He should touch them, and His disoiples rebuked those that brought them; but when Jesus saw it. he was much displeased, and said unto thorn suffer the little children to come unto Me and forbid them not: for of such is the kingdom of God. Verily I say unto you, a hosoever shall not receive the kingdom of God as a little child he shall not enter therein. And He took them up in His arms put Eti3 hands upon them ane blessed them." This the rev. preacher divided into three panels : The interest of Christ in all children ; the consecration of child life ; and the full revelation of the glory of childhood. The</p>
        <p>i-nt;:reet of Christ in children was Manifested when some of them were brought to situ and Heitook them up in His arms. When did man's interest in anything he bad made begin ? After he had finished with it? Nay, as • soon as ever be had conceived it. And so, God's interest in child life was when II first of all had gave it life. The children belonged to God from the very beginning. They came from God and went beck to God. They should tell them that just Rs Jacob's ladder rested on earth, and the top of it in heaven, with the angels ascending and descrnding on it, with a circular m vn merit between the heaven and the earth, so the child first came from heaven, and after staying awhile on eirth went back to dol. Christ was the Guardian of the child, and gave the world a new gospel ef childhood. He asked them to sustain the %cork of those who sus• tamed the wail{ of the lease of the teachers Sunday evening sermon .was funded on Matt.</p>
        <p>- , x 29 " F.ar, not them- which kII the body, but are not ' able to kill the soul ; 'but ra!her fetr him which Is able to destroy both Soul and b Ay." Moat powerfully Mr. Goodman impressed upm his hrvers the value and immortality of the soul, and brought his Subject to dovetail beautifully with tbe importance of Sunday School Work. It was. perhaps the Monday evening serm&lt;m wh:ch proved the best of the series. Taking for tin text ff And maan the top of the pillarn was lih-avafk" 1 Kings vii., 22, the same preacher illueratr d how the lily coo and pm grartate with which the Oran; of Solomon's Tempe were decorated represented the dethfication or all that wag beat and purest to the services o 7 the Almiztitv, and exhorted his hearers to give of their best to he glory of God, _ _</p>
        <p>The musical portion of the service was eminently 'me- cessful as a whole. and rrfizeted Rreat credit upon Mr J W Temperler, the oreanl4 at Camden, who trainer] the rhoir Ind soholare. The solo on Sunday evenine by Mies Nellie Temperler, °' The ylice of hope." was lalf`aa- inely rendered, and referred to in sui'sble terms by the I Ws' 3 H Goodman on illorithy roght. Misses Shaw (selarano), and Lambert (contralo) were els° effective in the refrain " All is well." Had the humming poem°</p>
        <p>of the chorus been unaccompanied and tee chorus not quite so heavy, the effect would have been considerably enhanced, but even as rendered all had a very pleasing offect. Taken as a whole, however, the services were succes.ful and hmrtv.—The collections realised £36 7s, which is a decrease on hut year a figure.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-04-26_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>DETERMINED SUICIDE OF A BURTONWOOD FARMER'</head>
        <p>DETERMINED SUICIDE OF A BURTONWOOD FARMER'</p>
        <p>Considerable consternation was created in the neigh- bourhood cf Buttonwood on Sunday morning by the suicide of a farmer n .med Joseph Newick, who drowned himself in a most determined manner. It appears that Newick was the tenant of the Wneat Sheaf Farm,on the boundary of Bold and Buttonwood. Some financial trouble seems to have preyed upon his mind, and all Sunday morning be behaved in a very strange manner. About a quarter-past eleven he said " Good-bye " to several people on the farm, ani imm,d•ately ran out across a field to a pit about 200 yards from his hense.A couple of labourers followed at the request of the house- keeper, but failed to overtake him, and he was discover- ed in the middle of the pit quite dead. Dr Casey, of St Helens Junction, was called,but prgrounced life extinct. Deceased, who was about 32 years of age, was a single man, and sass well-known in the di.ttrict. It is stated that he had threatened to take away his life on two other occasions that morning, and at other timcs prior o the tragic event. Mr S Brighouse, county coroner, conducted the inquest at the Chapel House Inn, Buttonwood, on Wed- nesday night, on the body o' Joseph Newick, Cermet, of Whearacre Farm, Buttonwood, who drowned himself on Sunday morning last.—The evidence showed that the ceased was despondent on account of financial diffi. culties, and wished he was out of the way.—T: e jury returned a verdict of "Suicide whilst of unsound mind."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-04-26_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>WARRIN!GTON.</head>
        <p>WARRIN!GTON.</p>
        <p>P.S.A at the Y.M.C.A. An open P.S.A. service was held on Sunday after noon when there was a very full attendance, Mr G . Savage occupied the chair. The Rev J F Matthews gave an address on the "Art of Christian Living,' and Miss M Collin a fine rendering of " Rock of Ages." Mr W Woods sang in excellent style "The Sailor's Grave." The President and Vice-President. also took part in the proceeding.. Pictorial Post Carcts. English and Poreign View Cards, highly Artistic Cards and all luck's Card, for the £l,OOO Prize Competition Selecti Ins sent on receipt of deposit or references by HUGO LING AND Co.. 14 Church-street, Li verpool,—Alvt. Buckley-street Chapel Organ Fund. Mrs Simpson is to be congratulated upon the sue. cess which atteeded the concert promoted by her in aid of the Buckley-street Chapel Organ Fund, which event took place in the Co-operative Hall, Winwick-street. on Monday evening. The room was filled to its utmost capacity and each item in a first-rate programme was received with every manifestation of approval. Mr R A Naylor who was accompanied by Rise Naylor occupied the chair ard in the course of a brief address explained the objt of the concert. The major portion of the programme was sustained by Mr Tom Boothroyd's banjo, mandoline and guitar orchestra, in a manner which reflectee, fluite credit upon the able con- ductor. Two members of the o-chestra gave indi- vidual selections, Miss Leonore Nolland con- tributing the mandoline solo "Killarney," with taste and skill, and Mr Herbert Butcher displaying considerable ability in the violin solo Traviata " The other artists who appeared were Miss Dorothy Truelove, Miss Annie Bate, Air Horace Atkinson, and Mr Harry Broadbent Miss Truelove acquitted herself with credit in the "Jap- anese love song," and " An old sun dial," both of which called forth cordial applause. Miss Bete was heard to advantage in "Flight of ages," and "Angus McDonald,"and Mr Horace Atkinson's contribution., "The raft "and " The skipper" were well received. The humour of th i evening was supplied by Mr Broadbent, whose efforts to amuge were highly successful. Mr A Trow discharged the d aties of accompanist, The usual votes of thanks brought the proceedings to a close. Annual Billiard Handicaps at the Conservative Club. The two billiard handicap which have been in progress at the above club for several months were brought to a conclusion on Tuesday evening, when the remaining players, Mr J Unsworth and Mr F Redfern, tried conclusions in the second-class billiard-room in the presence of a large number of spectators. The game was watched with consider- able interest, as both gentlemen are good players, but in the end victory rested with Mr Unswortb, who was warmly applauded. His largest break was 35. The prize-winners are as follows :--First-class room: 1, T Horrohin, son. ; 2, T Potter; 3,8 Grant: 4, R N FoFtv • 5, F Redfern; 6, ft Forster; 7, F Aukland ; 8. 1 .traw; 9. E Ball; 10, L 11, George Platt; 12. J Podmore ; 13, W Reed. Second-class room : 1, J Unsworth ; 2, r R,edfern; 3, L Bolton; 4, S Howarth ; 5, J Pinnington ; 6, C Unsworth ; 7, T Sutton; 8, P Randall; 9,W Warren; 10, E Fowler; 11, A Bate; 12, J Unswerth, jun. ; 13, C Oldfield ; 14, E Morris; 15, A G Wallington: 16, D Rawling.—Great praise is due to Mr James S*rettle, who has acted as hen secretary to the Handicap Committee; to Mr tt W Daintith, hen treasurer; and to Mr John Cox (chairman of the committee). whose arrangements throughout the handicn 1.f.::0 been most satisfactory.—The prizes were sub,eqaently distributed by Alderman Francomb. CADBURY'S COCOA has a world-wide reputation as S4ot CiOUS, strengthening beverage, ei.d a valuable nutritive to 4 the ..Lancet" says it represents the standard of highest purity." Always insist on having CADRITRY'S—soId Only in packets and Tins—as other Cocoas are often substituted fog the make of estra profit —Advt. (2) Singular Local Action at the Assizes. In the Nisi Priva Court,at the Manchester Assizes on Tuesdai , an action was brought by Sir Thomas )annell, engin-er, of Old Trafford, formerly of ly-latie. Warrington, against Mr Ileyl Die, ging direeter of the St Helens Cable Company Limited, B ink Quay, Warrington. for the return of a transfer of ten .£lO fully paid preerence shares in the St Helens Cable Covipany. As sufficient jury- men could not be found in the building, counsel for both sides consented to the action being tried by the judge alone. Mr Acton (u.structeri by Mr Artbur Browne), appeared for plaintiff, and Mr Shea, KC, and Mr Atkinson instructed by Messrs Grandy Kershaw. and Company, of Manchester), apreared for the defendant.—The plaintiff's ease was that be was engaged by the defendant to assist in carrying on the business of the company at a salary of .£4 a week. Mr Dia after. wards suggested to the plaintiff that he should only pay him .E,3 a week, and that the arrears of .£1 per week should be paid at the end of each month. Plaintiff applied for the arrears in January, 1900, and then the defendant said that in return for the services he had rendered it was his intention tct present him with tin fully paid .£lO shares in the company. In March he handed to the plaintiff for signature a document which he (plaintiff) believed was a certificate for the shares, and said that he must wign it. Later on •he defendant again saw the plaintiff, and asked the later to give him the doCu. merit as the a area would require to ba registered. Plaintiff handed the certificate to the defendant, and it had never been returned to hiaa.—T'le de- fence was that the shares welt) to become the pro- perty of the plaintiff if he gave satisfaction during his term of employment. The defendant alleged that he did not give sati. faction, and was thorefore. not, entitled to the Aar • Justice Darling, found for the plaintiff, and gave judgment for £2OO to be reduce Ito a farthing on the delivery to plaintiff of a certificate for ten .£lO fully paid shares in the company. ICITCH.RE RANUES. :Tile Grates Marble and Wood Chimnsv pißcis J. 3. ou.syn,slto 55.Rrklze-st—A.cle LADIES zy WANT OF HELP should use MMUS'S IrmiulTUßE CREAM, 9d. and Is. fraun The Chemist, in Bridge-street. WarrE Iton —lidvt</p>
