      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-04-26_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>BETTER BE SAFE THAN SORRY.</head>
        <p>BETTER BE SAFE THAN SORRY.</p>
        <p>--- In the study of human affairs around us, offering the Very greatest diversity of circumstance' relating to physical troubles and sufferiog, there is always She temptation to say that -luck or chance bas much to do with such matters. Olathe othet band,if we gee a man, hale and hearty, and well no in years, we lay "Be wears well," little thinking of the carefulness, ths watchfulness, and the adapting of means to an end, that such a man exercises over himself. Then, again, we may hear of some wonderful cure effected, at a time when the patient's case seemed tropeless,how incredulou we often are at such times, and if overtaken by illness ws are apt to go on in the old humdrum w sp. just be- cause of our unbelief,but depend upon it, yo d had better be safe than sorry, and if you are suffering from general 'weakness, nervousness, and prostration, with disinelina- Mos to work, without being downright ii, the safest, the surest,and the most satisfactory remedy is "Gwilym 'Evans' " Quinine Bitters. Its wonderfol r 3mers have proved marvellous in hundreds of cases. Wh'in purchas- ing be sure you find the name" Gwilym Es sns "on the label, stamp, and bottle, without whies none are genuine. Sold in bottles 2s 9d And 4s 61, or in Oases of three 4s 6(.1 bottler( fez 124 61, of all Patent. Medicine Vendors are] Chemists, or direct, carriage free, from the Quinine Bitters Manufacturing Company, Limited,' Ltanelly, South Wales.</p>
