        <p>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES MINOR OASES. Wa rrington Borough Court.</p>
        <p>V oar. —Before Cogneillnr Bmethnn2t (in the °hair), COUI3.</p>
        <p>cillor John Roberts, and Mr. Thornton. Gems CRUELTY To A H01t50.-John Williimson Parker. 87. SE Mary-street. was charged with ill-treating a horse by werkiog it whilst in an unfit state on the 9-11 hest -Inspec- tor Print, R-S P C.A., stated that he was on duty in Rnut- ford•read, and saw the defendant driving black golfing attached toe lorry laden with betties and jars of ginger beer. otietng that the beetle was lame he stopped it and examined it. There was a wound under the collar. which bad not been eased. lbe collar was pressing on the sore, and matter Was being discharged. There were wounds on the knee, and forelimb, and there was els() .5 very. large supporating sore on the hip, full. the size of the palm of his !witness's) hand. On the off. lie there were two wounds under the collar, the size of a tw. shilling and one sffillieg piece respectively. Witness also found a wound on the ehoulder aud one on the fetlock which was quite the size of a five shilling :lees. The animal Woe in very poor condition and was suffering great pain.lt was quite unfit for work. In justice to the defendant he mentioned that Parker destroyed the horse aft rwards.-P C. Bennett gave corro- borative evidence-The Chief Constable stated that In July last, at Daresburv. the, defendant was fine I 2.0 s and costs in two oases of causing horses to be 'worked whilat in an unfit state.-The magistrates imposed a fine of 201 and. coat'. Tian CARE or It B. FURNESS -AIIoTHER APPLicerron.- Bert ha Furness, 348, Knutsford-road, Was summoned to show close why a maintenance order made on the 23rd November, 19CO, should not he reduced, her husband. Harry Bolton Furness stating that he was unable to pay the amount.-The applicant ended that he wee at present earning 131 per week and could not therefore afford to pay the amount grante 1 in the order (El. per week). He asked for th• order to be re- duced to 8s per week-The Bench adjourned the ease for a week in order that defendant might produce corroborative evidence as to the amount of his earnings. maalaa A • PLEAS TTie CRAIMED wiTli ASSAULT -Thomas Clare. 3 Packet-stage. who es seventy-two years of age. and it was stated .W 1 been paralysed for 14 years and had to be wheeled into the court in a bath chair. was summoned for assaulting Emily Moore. 15 Mersey-street, on the 13th April.-Moore was. charter] with assalting Matilda Smethurst, 3. Packet- stagei eta the same. time -Mr Browne appeared on behalf- of Mrs ktoore,and the defendant and the complainent Smethure- were represented by Mr Steel.-Mr Browne ate ed that inthe' date in question Mrs Moore bad occasion to pass the door of the:defendants house and he called to her. to go in. blie did so and found the man lying on the sofa. He made uswe,at. some , disgusting expression towards her whialk. she resented, and he then Struck her thre)th times on the - arm with a stick. The woman Smethurst. who acted as housekeeper for the defenclant,earne in and,defendnit who had managed to get off the sofa held the doer whilst Smethurst thrashed Mrs Moore. ?des Illoore's nu band then entered the house and separated them. eltorl the defence Mr Steel said that Mrs Moore went to the house with-. the intention of piekine se quarrel. She called the' defendant an old cripple and said The would make him fanse., lie told her hap away and she refused so he threw a Pee" water oyer her. He denied having struck her with the glee.. Mrs Smethurst then came in and Mrs Moore struck her awl, atm threw a knife at her. She dragged the des'emlant off the Ws and kicked him on the leg, in aim ing a se erre •wound.-* Tlw Bench Considere i there nal been a great deal of false swearing and dismissed b)tn cases. MONDAY.-Ilefore the Mayor • (in Lbe chair). Aldermen Hntehinson and Francomb, Councillor Smethurst and Messrs F' Monks, J White, J Platt, Bin-shall, Hayward, Wright. and Thorn] oil. , bIot:DENT, Snot, Dooß Tao Warlitirtoll.:B4. Steup-street, and William Sloane, 23. Crowder-street. were charged with stealing two men's cardigan jenk.ets. twe enats. and two vest*, valued at Et 17s. the propeLtd: Of Messrs G H ad H B Jackson. Winwiek-road. at 7.55 on Saturday night. -Beth pleaded guilty. -The Chief Constable said that about 7 At on Saturday a young man named Holbrook saw the man Warburton take some clothing and other articles from the in- side of the doorway of the shop and run away. Superintend- eat Monaghan afterwards saw one of the men wearing a stiolen jacket standing outside a pawnshop. lie took him inside the shop and found his cemoinion trying to pawn the oincr clothes. Both men acre brought to the Pollee Station Whets charged Sloane said that Warburton took them.' War- burton had no hing to say -Both men new stated that they were in drink at the time -The Bench fined elefeadents 20s eed create, with the option of a month's Imprisoramenh with- rd labour TWo MCjNIUSFoR Ditalcarton. -Patrle'c 'Riley, n tabourer., late; working at etilgia, was charged with meaning awot from the boronsh on the ttts December. leavinig his wife and four children Chargeable , to tse n funds of the Union. Kr Shearer mid that on the, date in Question prisoner left town taking with film hisesesek's wages. He had been away almost, five mon:hs Pn Sunday he was Reprehended at Wigan. The family bad reetr.the Union £ll se-Prisoner was committed to gaol Los two mosths with bard labour. . , - ONZE-RVI:LT 0- F,:go Wointoß.-Joseph Taylor, 13. Adminsi-street ; John kloston, 35. Lord Nelson-street ; Reward 7)evies. 10, Admiral-street were charged:with tossing of coins in Admiral-street on Sunday. P.°, Clough said that in consequence of reports haying ,been made he went in plain clothes to Admiral-street and saw the three detendants tossing for money. He minght the first defendant and obtained the names and addresses of the others. INumerous complaints had been received of gambling on Sunday, but the police had great difficulty in ..arresting the offenders, who employed" scouts "-The elite Constable ,stated that. with the fine weather the gambline semen had Commenced and he asked the Bench to inflict suah a penalty as would be a warning to others-A fine of 5s and costs was 'lmposed on each defendant. • 7 ALLEGED CRUELTY To • HORSE -THE CASE DISMDRErw -John Mona en, of Orford lane, John Cleaver, 28.1 Blizebeth-street, and John Connolly, 39, Rose and Crown. street, were Summoned at the instance of the R.5.P.C.a..-1 'the first-named for cruelty to a horse by working it while In ' 'an unfit state, and the two others for causing it to be worked. -Mr B A Connor appeared for the defendants, and pleaded not guilty, -Inspector Print 'said he saw a coople of horse., attached to a 'bus belonging to Mr Edward Connor, in Bridge-street About eleven o'clock on the night of Mare's 30th The near side animal was lame He told McMullen. who was in charge, to take the horse to the stables and he did so. its said he did not snow the animal was lame. Cleaver ad- mitted at the stables afterwards that the home Was a hit stiff on the near fore limb, but that be 'did not know it was so bad. 7 he third defendant was the foreman of the yard. John' , veterinary surgeon.said he examined tLt. here, on the Intl inst. It had a eont meted tendon. It was not fit for the • -work te. stopping and starting a 'bus. With rest and change of occupation it would be fit for ssork.-Cross-examinect : The • horse was not necessarily in pain.- For the defence, Mr ' Eplained that though the animal was lame in the te.daniesi sente of the word used by vets, it did not follow, that It suffered any pain or was unfit for work, The ease was dismissed. AIMIHF.II CIIANCE.-14.1argaret Oakes, 1 Dial-court and Sarah Ellen RObinson. 45, Winwlek.road, were both charged -with commit tine a breech of the peace in Winwick-road on the 16th inst.-P.C. Mtn kett hayir g proved the case, the Chief Constable laid he would ask for an adjournment for a mantle This WAS Oak's 23rd appearance. An effort was being made to reclaim her. Whether it would b. Su, -- fal or not he ' could not say, but could only hope. lie w ,o 1 i withdraw the el-large agefn.t Robluson.-This Was ellowed end the ease ' against Oakes was adjourned for a tennth.</p>
