      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-04-26_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>Runcorn Petty Sessions.</head>
        <p>Runcorn Petty Sessions.</p>
        <p>Before Messrs F 3 Norman. A R Norman. and Eli Posnett, PITCH-AND-TO5B "—William Martin, of Glibert-straet.the missing defendant of last week, in the case of the police v. a number of youths of Gilbert-street for gaming with coins on the llth April, three of whom were fined 10s, pleaded not guilty.—The Bench, however, considered the evidence of Constables Bowyer and Gilpin concl,.sive evidence, and fined Martin 10.—The reason he did not appear on the first occa- sion was due to his having been obliged to go with his boat to Liverpool. A Row AT THE THEATRE —James Williamson pleaded guilty to being drunk and disorderly In Duke-street,Runcona the previous Saturday night.— Constable Shaw stated that Thomas Steele, t the check-taker at the Theatre Royal mileii him in to eject Williamson who was creating a disturbance, This the constable did, and Williamson was sent on his way home. He returned, however, and ere +ted a disturbance in the street, which resulted in his being summoned.—Thls being Williamson's B.st offence. he was fined to only.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-04-26_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>AN EX-POLICEMAN'S APPLICA-</head>
        <p>AN EX-POLICEMAN'S APPLICA-</p>
        <p>TION.</p>
        <p>A Question of Public Morality and, Policy."</p>
        <p>Temporary Authority Refused.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Police Court, on Monday, before the Mayor and other magistrates, Mr. A. Browne applied on behalf of James Worrell, for temporary authority to sell at outdoor beer licensed premises in Hoyle-street, off Bewsey-road. He said he might as well be perfectly candid, because it was bound to come out, and inform the magistrates that Mr. Worrell was not free from con- victioe under the Licensing Act, but be thought he would be able to ccnvin:e them that the convictions were made under such circumstances as justified them in declining to further punish him upon those grounds. The licence at present belonged to Mr. Robert Tickle ; who was Mr. WorralPs father-in-law. Before she married, Mrs. Worrell held the licence for seven years in her own name. Mr Worrell served twelve years in the Warrington Police Force. H. left the Force as a Warrant °nicer and for 20 years kept the Bewsey Inn in Pitt- street, and then went to the Bridge lan, Frothham. That was in September, 1899. During the whole time be was m :Warricgroe there was nothing whatever against him. The only thing co be considered now was were the two convictions at Frodsham for permitting drunkenness sufficient to jus if y the magistrates in say- , ing that neither Mr Wori all nor his wife was a fit person to have this licence. The house which Mr. Worrell held at Frodsham was a peculiar house, being the first from the chemical works at Weston. On the first Occasion he was lined 208 and costs for permitting drunkenness and a similar fine 15 months afterwards for a similar offence. The latter conviction was a surprise to every- one in the court. It was a paltry case. . The Chief ConstaVe lam going to object to this application and Mr Browne is not entitled to criticise the decision of another Bench. _ _ _</p>
        <p>The Mayor (to Mr Browne): 1 cannot see how you can conscientiously ask us to grant the licence to a man who has been twice convicted for the very serious offnce of permitting drunkenness. Mr Browne: The penalties were only 20s in each case and the licence was not endorsed.</p>
        <p>The Mayor: It is the offence itself. Mr Browne said the matter was one for the considera- tion of the whole Bench. Mr Worrell had a 32 years' good character in Warrington. He was an old man now, unfit for manual labour. If they would not grant the licence th him he (Mr Browne) would ask for it to be granted to his wife. She had the licence before the was married for seven years. The Mayor said the circumstance now were entirely different. She had now taken over the responsibilities ,of laer husband_ He could tell Mr Browne for his own I info; mation that the conduct of the la mem in Pitt-street was notoriously bad although the police took no action for years. The Chief Constable said that was before his time. Mr Browne • If one has these personal recollec• Lions— .</p>
        <p>- _ The Chief Constable said that Mr Browne had brought them out. Mr Browne observed that there was no conviction and the licence was never refused. He made his application in the terms he had stated. .</p>
        <p>The Mayor said that this was an attempt to get a man into a house who had already been twice convicted by a little family arrangement. Mr Browne: It is nothing of the kind. The fact must be taken into consideration that the licence of the house at Frodsham was renewed. _ _</p>
        <p>- Mr Wright Had the licence been endorsed? Mr Browne : No. The Mayor said they could:not consent to an applica- tion made on these terms. It was most irregular. There WM a regular and irregular way. Mr Browne might bring half a dozen people in. He must make the ap- plication either for Mr or Mrs Worrell. • ' Mr. Browne :—I apply for Mrs Worrell against whom there is nothkg whatever. When her father dies she Will be the owner. The Chief Constable objected to the application.—He said he would hav&amp;-objected to Mr Worrell because he had been tivrce convicted in fifteen months. The ap- pfication made in regard to Mrs Worrell was similar to s. greet Many applications made in that court and re- fused en the around that it was an attempt, as the ,Mayor bad said, to put an objectionable tenant in by e little family arrangement. To grant the license to Mrs Worrell would be contrary to the usual practise and /Jolley el the court and would be to practically grant, what Mr Browne had not the courage to apply for, the licence to Mr Worrell. He would manage the place for his wife. If the magistrates granted this application be did not know where they were going to draw the line. It would not leave him much ground in opposing any similar application. Be know that Mr Worrell had a good character in Warrington and he was extremely sorry to have to make this objection. The granting of an application of this kind, however, was not a question of charity but of public morality and' policy. The Magistrates retired to consider their decision and on their return into court, the Mayor said they had de- cided to refine the transfer.</p>
