      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-04-26_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL. Warrington Team secures the Warrington Cup</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL. Warrington Team secures the Warrington Cup</p>
        <p>Interesting Match at EarleStown. Much interest centred in the final tie in the Wellington Cup Competition, which took piaci on the Earlestown Club's ground on Saturday aftertioon. The opposing 'teams were Warrington and Newtende-Willows, and the line weather was responsible for a splendid gate. It was 'oomphted that over 1.500 persees bid j annoyed from Warrington, and Newton claitivd 1,000 supporters. The total assembly weell not fall far etiort of 3,000. The teems lined out as fellows : Warrineton G Aspinall •' full backe, LI Cook and T Dilworth ; half- backs, J Bryan, J Murray (captain), and T Ellis; for- wards, R Pierpoint, E Ball, le Riley, J Copeland, and L finerples. Newton :--tioal, A Horrocks ; full backs, J /leiter' and J Raynor ; half-backs, J Morrissey, G Houghton, and G Worsley ; forwards, P II Kerney. R Lee, T Turton, C Hill, and W Billington. Haring lost, the toss, Tinton 'started off for Newton, who playe with the sun in their eyes, and against the ,wind, but downhill. Newton' at 'once raced awey, but were immediately 'checked by B•lan. Not td be deaied Newtoel still atticked,..but Cook relieved with. a huge kick.' Copeland !teemed a nice pass, but was puliea up, being off-side. ' 'Play waa sharp, thoueh beth ,teapie were suffering from " cup tic" excitement. Horrocks was called upon to save, clearing in capital style. Sharpies, however, was on the alert, and collared the ball, but' had the misfortune to send bellied. Newton were awarded a foul, but Dilworth headed away., After play had been contested in the Newton territory, Hill raced down and centred well, but Turton kicked dead. Warrington immediately raced sway, and from a unwanted passing movement Riley shot in, Elorrocks cleared, but Sharpies met the ball, and, running up, , scored the first point with a beautiful " daisy-cutter.' Warringten continued to press, but Newton returned, Cook clearing. 'Pretty,cornbined play between Hill and Lee was a feature, but Dilworth rushed up and effected a splendid clearance. Kenny made a capital run on the left, and forced a corner, het Aspinall cleared in splen- did style. Play was now contested In midfield between the two sets of forwards. From a concerted passing movement Riley peeved t Sharpies. who sent outside. Turton cleared the ball at the ex eense of a corner,which was well placed, Horrocks senitng over. Pierpoint received a nasty knock the gltllP teeing delayed five minutes. Before' he resumed, Newton raced down and Hilt essayed a futile shot at val. After 20 minutes' play Kenny equalised amidst enthusiaem•From the kick- off Warrington pressed, but were beaten back, Ellis clearing. Half-time arrived with the score cue each On resumption Warrington gralually wore the Necvtonians down, and afeer quarter-of-an-hour's play. Sharpies raced down, and passing to Pierpoint, that player headed through amidst unparalleled enthusiasm. Excitement ran high, and when from a capital rue Pierpoint scored the third goal the issue was never in doubt. Newton pressed in turn. but retired a thoroughly beaten team by three goals to ewe The cheering which greeted the conclusion of the match showed that the victory Ives a most popular (mu. Mr.lsberwood presented the cup to Merray the captain of the winning team, Murray, who V 745 vec,fereusly cheered, ;hen received the cup and said the team had made their minds up on winning the cup, (Cheers,) The Rev H G Roberts, who also was cheered, saint as a supporter of the Warrington As9othtiett football team, be had wee greafotrptlheeasmuraenliynwoaffyeirninwghbicihs tcheeny- tgor atthuel aNt i.own st n notteaomnty, to the Warringt, had fought their battle. (Cheers.) The players then left the Ee'd , Murray shoulder high On arriving et the team's dressine-rome the cup was filled nod the hsalth of the Wallington team drunk. The winners drove home, and mule a tour of the town being bailed with eb:'ers en rrit-o.</p>
