        <head>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>"A' TEAM COMPETITION</p>
        <p>-- - Runcorn A. v. Wigan A. This fixture which was p men at Irwell-lane, on Saturday, provcd a very orle-.3idmi gar-o And resulted in a win for BUI2COIII 4 by 60 prints TOOfateness of the fixture was due to the ofubs heieg vnaMs to meet on the original date.</p>
        <p>r</p>
        <p>tetters to tbe Odor. ---- NOTICK TO CORRESPONDENTS.</p>
        <p>tem intended for publication must in all cases send neir names and addresses to the</p>
        <p>Editor. Matters introided for poblicstion must bp written on one side oalw nf the paper and sent in. if possible, not latter than Thursday. Correspondents are strongly advised to write britfly. A short letter has much greater chance of</p>
        <p>insertion than a long one. We cannot under ordinary circumstances under- take to return the manuscripts which ate not used.</p>
        <p>Mercury and the Trades Council. Sir.—" Mercury " has told me what I knew ri.garding the Law ,'(Elective Auditoeship, and also afforded some needed proof of his intelligence. It is evident he knows something; but some of his arguments are not intelli- gent nor logical. For iestance he says" Labour should be sdiquately represented," yet all along be has been attemptine to trounce the Trades Council for forwarding labour representation. And the Council is the only organized labour amalgamation from which such action can come. It is eviiient also he does not know the difficulties of labour candidature. " Mercury" soars to the clouds in his indignation at my supposed gross libel of workingmen (in charging them with apathy and indifference). fie had better descend to sober fact. My experience is, and it is the eXperiet es of all,—except " Mercury,"—witti whom I have apceen, that workingmen are their own enemies That thi y are blinded through party prejudice, and many of them will sell their votes for beer. Tnat they have the power to alter for good many things but the desire is lacking either through want of knowledge or apathy. I do not believe in flattery, and I always try to speak that which I believe to be true. To return to trio Elective Anditorstilp. The intention of the Legislature wss to provide a safeguard for the , ratepayers by necessitating the election of two auditors Now the interpretation of the law differs widely, as witness our own recent locel discussion. My point was that workingmen auditors—not necessarily stove-grind- ers—have in ocher towns by a broad interpretation of their duties in that office, and by their public-Spirited action in reporting direct to those who elected them, bad • good effect towards purity in local affairs ; both by exposing jobbery, and in arousing keen interest in the mass of the electorate. If in other towns why not in Warrington ? Mr Flanagan, according to "Mercury," would have been 2 round peg in a square hole, for, says he, we need "qualified men," viz., accountants. What about two of our present auditors. who art' not so qualified? Are they round pegs also? It would appear so according to " Mercury." " Mercury " denies in tote the connection between Trade Unionism and Elective Auditorshlp. Labourers,' in the widest sense of the term, are the great mej irity of ratepayers. Labour is organised in societies. These societies are affiliated with the Trades Council. Just as the head of a family bringing home the income sees to its wise expende ure, so the Trades Council, as the voice of organised labour, is justified in nominating and sup- porting a candidate for an office which sees to the spending of the rates. " Mercury's " "large number of intelligent working men who refrain from joining the Council because cktif its action "exist only in his imagination, nor will the Council receive advice from anyone lacking experience and service. " Mercury "acknowledges his trifling error about the nunster of Guar: tans Warriogton erects, but he cslmly ignores my challeoge to prove his statement about the large number of Insurance agents who speak at the meetings of the Cluncil. This mis-stacement he has twice repeated and has not the moral courage to apologise to the body he is attempting to criticizo. He also insinuates I am not speaking the truth re Mr Flanagan's election expenses althceseee the fact 10 as I state it. I cannot compliment hint on his closing remarks about "Demagogues." It must have been &amp; "windy spasm," for it j5l entirely out of place in this discussion and it does not improve his letter, for language of that sort is the last rubbishy resort of all who lack argument. Yours, P. J.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Your cultured and gentlemanly correspendent "Mercury " evidently has no intention to allow ,me to retire peacefully from this " aimless " controversy. I have tried to slide out of it with as much grace as my nature will bend to, I hsve also said in * former letter that notleni creditable, wia to be gained by further argument with , a person, who lacks the rudimentary elements of courtesy towards his opponents. who aesp makes statements, knowing full well when be made I them, etiatlthey were untruthful, and were evidentlyl made with the intention of creating a :wrong impression amongst a certain erietion.bLyoruregeteeereeor electeral, purposes.' Ia his II qt letter. for want of so:netblieer better, he resurrected a subject that had been buried for four mouths, and in which he took no part, and aceused me cii making statements that were " grossie false." I ' called upon him if tm had the instincts of a gentleman , to prove the fats hiod or apolofze, lie has done neitber, but with a modesty more discreet than gentlemanly 13f3 entirely igpores the subject.' have accrued him of being . . entirely ignorant of the subPcts upon which he chose Ito enlighten year readers; for litetatme; what" Mercury " is pleased to term a sight mistake with regard eto 1 the number of candidates, may. ,appsearivrtelliing I now, but at the time itawartinatiee elereteikee. jelPeietrate his complete and 'resaleable "egnorance u ~cennecd- on with ea Metter that he himself e intrcidueed., 'Again the -Trades Council, did not run Or insurance agents for Beards of Guardians. and as 1, vie previeuelY said the' i Trades Commie did net " n Mr 'Flanagan for 1 Elective Auditor" not sma any Union or Trades , Council contribute a peony ' towards my election ' lexpensee, further the " .Secnie e Grinder " himself did, not even contribute 1 tie farthing towards the, ,cest. As " Mercury iecldires 'my assurance nn this I 'Point, Permit me to inform . him that " Per." is Iperfectly correct. all expense e ;were . collected from , and paid by workingmen. some et whom have known' tne for quite a number of years and have, Worked with me in other parte ne,the countryeand are therefore more ' (ratified to judge as to mye! fitrigeseueet..ebleitY_" than " Mercury " who, in ' all probibilitirelies'nWereeVen 1 spoken to me, Now, Sire' am *boa to enter upen another phase of !hie iiiteiesting eubject, and I due° with great retuct- se co as I hate to drag otheepeophel (Into me arguments to ancipner them, but as term in a meener driven to it to defend my own position, poreepe oxernae, be .mused for the liberty I take: In substance. b' Mercury " says, there is no connection between a" Siren Grinder "and an Auditor, therefore the former is incapable of being' the latter. I admit that eeiegele is wide, but does not the same method of readeping , apples in the case of a " Tailor " and an Auditor, yetlbeee'of *Cr anditors is a tailor. I will Poen 'go further' and enquire . what] inrellectuel cennection la there.' between a thoemaker I ied is Mayor e yet the Mayor ot thie borough was a shoemaker, end evidently takes a pride in confessing it. Still in his will in'tenslateney " ,adeicary " approves of , the two lateee, eracleeirleni and ,nouderees the " Stove 1 Grinder," tut while I readiiv admit that. " Grinders" as le body are not overburdeneel;wlth intelligence. I must remind l' elererery "that the late" 'teed Chief Justice Russell, said the ' dame thing about- our hereditary leeisletors:in the House of Lords. Let me also remind " Mercury " that it by no meaes follows, because one who i born of Poor parents and has it ,veteettender s years, bad to rassist his parents in keeping the., ‘et Wolf from the door" arid in cainequence has leernedra ieea. lat the ',remise of Is education, that .ench,e.- emu} , a years burry on is incepable of any furthereedecaetna deilislopment. Tot ench has been toy experieece. T_#' hivi hill to .evork,bard for every penny of the money that Wel for whatever education I may lay". still withal the above diarievautages I ' have . held; apse from ine4 trade, .entne geod presittrina both ia'and'but of her late Majestv's.rermee. I etin in Beeseasionta a certificate, signed -by the late General Dillon, lecorreneeeene, me divisional clerk, and expert book 'keeper,' 1 another froro the War Office as " Trained Musician.", while I have another from the Rev ,Gibson Grogson. commending eme for the „Manner, i 4 which I kept the hooks of the" Soldiers Total Abseirience Association in India." Yet I ana Coed be " Mercury "in his limp and tabby letters that I em not as capable of running up a column of figures as a "tailor.' whose sole recommend. etion hangs upon tba fact ef hie being a member of a powerful political organisation. I have written the above bit of personal history, not for the particular gratification a yew correspondent. but for the benefit Of your readers who do not know me pereonalty,and that ' they may judse between me and the irrepressible Mercury, who seems to me to be endowed with his full share of that charity, better known as " Christian Charity,' that would grovel at the feet of and lick any- thing in the shape of a nisei, for the sake of the position he occupies, while the mere workman who would dare to assert his independence, and stand manfully to his principles as e Man, he would =shag remorselessly , as he would a beetle. • In concluaion eiretiet ice say this, that whoever is at the other end of the weres to inspire " Mercury" there is one thing quite clear, like the cowardly Boer with his white flag, he prefers to fight from a position of comparative security, that his antagonist has little or no chancti of reaching. In other words, in making his first public attack on me, he was aware that ho made it on a man who had the courage to foot his correspond once with his name and address, consequently he had , airy chance of heaping upon me public ridicule, while he himself ran no such risks,behind his "boulder." The above sir, is surely not a new version of the" Courage and love of fair play that all true Britons take a pride in procleiming wherever they are,or wherever they go." How win this meet the case, I do like a man that can stand boldly up And tell me at once what be feels, I can light with a lion that will rosr in my face But I can't hear a cur at my beels.—Oel Song. If " Mercury" has any claim to can himself a Beam and has the least spark of manliness in his nature, I call Upon hlm to come out from his " Boulder," and" take his Reuel "like a man and an Englishman, or, in future sign himself " Uoroitigated Cad."—Yours respectfully, MARTIN FLANAGAN. April 16th, 1901-</p>
        <p>trade 1 _</p>
        <p>Sir,—l again take the opportunity of replying to your correspondent" Mercury." Ele is to be complimented on the improved tone of his second letter and for the ingenious way in which hc avoids some of the cio tions which were raised in his first one, to which I and others , ' replied. It is true that in his letter of last week be admits one error, but in all fairness I think that. " Mercury "ought to have kept to the teat of his initial letter, or freely acknowle,tged the crtrrectintis to some of the statements he made. Ile said ttrAt the bulk of my first letter was compo&amp;ed of personal references, in lieu of argument, and qult es one sentence to prove this It is an easy matter to iay you're, another," but that is no argument at all. I invited him to allow you to publish his DAMP, instead of hiding his identity behind a nom-de-plume. Be not having done so, I take it that his modesty will not permit of hid] associating himself publicly with such convincing letters as be is credited with. He is still indignant at the action of the Trades Council in passing a resolution supporting the candidature of Mr frianag.tn for elective auditor. 1 must again inform " Mercury "the Council did net " run " Mr Flanagan nor bad they anything at all to do with his candidature further than the resolution named.</p>
        <p>Having seen 'a list of the subscribers to Mr Fi anag an's elec ion expenses, and all bills in connection therewith, I hope " Mercury ' will pardon me in saying " Pl." was quite correct in making the statement in the " Eximiner " that " Mr Fianagan's exp.-rises were paid by a number of working men." I claim that the Connell was just' fied in passing the resolution, if there was no other reason, but that of protesting against each political party having a represents: ive as Elective Auditor year after year, and using all the influence possible to prevent any outside candidate trona being elected. I admit that from the standpoint of experience an accountant is more , fitted for the work of awn' ing accoents than A "stove grinder." but the one who is most capable from a busi- ness standpoint is not al ways desirable from the rate- I payers' point of view. We have all known men who hive had exceptional business talents, but who as pub.</p>
        <p>lic servants, have n complete failures. A man with average ability and ;Intelligence, who is entirely free from business, social or political connections, whereby his actions may be influenced, is the one who is most likely to seri, i the ratepayers best. I cannot agree with t' Mercury "that public accountants are the most desir- able class of mentor the positiea of Elective Auditors,</p>
        <p>for however good their intentions may be there is a danger, that owing to their business and Bridal connee- Untie they may refrain from making public any matter which would be likely to implicate members of Town Councils and their friends. I am speaking generally now, lind do not wish to infer that the Elective Audi- tors for the borough will take such a course. At the same time, I am of the opinion that we ouebt not to elect them :o a position which leaves a possibility of their actions being iefluenced in the manner suggested. "Mercury" indulges in cheap sneers at the result of the Board of Guardiens election in Bewsey Ward, and seems delighted at the result. Let me tell him that we were not surprised alter learning what great odds we "bad to fight against. Mr Phaeton's opponent Wee about the strongest possible candirirte, being well-known Lthroughout the ward. He had the support of a strong cpolitiesil organisation, also a large number of workers and conveyances on the day of election:the &amp;premien in the Iron Trade was one of the things in his favour, but more powerful than all was the infl nonce of the 'trade.' On the other hand the Trades Council candidate was not so well known as his opponent, eepecially- among the ironworkers. We her] few workers, no conveyances and no pelitical erganisation to assist us. Other factors which told against our eandid ate, were the apathy of a large number of electors, even among trade-unionists, thetcemparative failure of the late member for the Ward as a Labour representative, and also the bet of him mending our opponent, oven after nominating the Trades Union candidate. Your correspondent "I'. J," fully justifies the con- stitution of the C admitting assurance agents and shop assistants as :fiembsrs of that body. The proportion of the two organisations is were' small, a fact which is to be regretted, when we know what conditions some ot them work under. We should be pleased to have the clerks of Warrington affiliated with us. It used to be a truism that clerks were too "respectable " to join a trades union, but the prejudice to organizetiote is sumly. if elowly,dying out. There is great peed for intelligent organization in every clads of lab nir. In histetter of last Saturday" Mercury "admits that labour should be adequately represented on I hr"l3oard." Why then complain abent the selection of Mr Plinston ? Your correspondent thir Its we could easily h vie nomin- ated another canaidate whose claim to be a direct repre- sentat ve of labour woidel hive been much bettergounded Had Mr Plinston been elected he would have rrpresented the " working deem of IVarrington" through the Trades Council and not the assurance agents. I fail to see why an engineer, joiner, wiredrawer, or , fatly otber Chief of tradesman" would have been a more suitable mode.. I date. If " Mercury" knew anything aboot Trades I Councils he would know that owing to the fact of em- ployers not being favourable to labopi4epresentation.on public bodies, there are few wrekingaien whe can offer themselves as candidates for eleo•ion. " Mercury" advises the Trades Council to keep to t,heir "legitimate enterprise." /3e however does pot tell us whit It the ''".legitimate enterprise ."' of a Trades • Ceuta. Perhaps your correspondent would have "Trades Unions to exist solely as sick and accident socie- ties, and not as fighting .orgsnizitions, and Trades Councils, meeting and paving Mous resolutions, on eoute labour ,qneetinn, Tipynit any intratinn ef carrying,thent out., Me seems to qbjent to them teking any politicep I nation. Let me tell tefereaty "and others of that' ilk ' that Trades Unions and Trades Councili tare deetinel to 'play an important part in the politicel life orient coun- try. Without wishing to lWel' the e working men of ' Warrineton'l hold that the majority bre very apathetic and indifferent towards local and even national elections, and they need tote awakened to a sense of their res- 1 ieoneibility and power. I hone the day te not far distant when the workers will realize the folly of dividing them selves into two parties at election times,the time above , all others, when they. oug.bt to he combined. ISTPW times demand new methods and we am living now under changing conditions. Trades Unions have accomplished Much both for Traded unionists and non-unionists.' The capitalists beveeve4 belie, begun to recognise the Power of organization , and are prepared to fight US with our own weapons. The power of 'the stbite has ' practically been destroyed for which we ought to be I thankful. The 1300.er of the workers now lies in their l votes, which if intelligently used will gain ,for .us wit is our legitimate edue—elf—sre_dentinge an the old- fashioned lines of .dividing oursely_en jetto two hostile I camps at election timed, WA shall' tatl'id Lfibirl what we and "fir ancestors have gained. Our duty then is to nee titer organiz +Along, ourluniiii; tid our voted for political purpotes. not for party politics, but for labour represen- tation on all local bodies and also in the House of Cots- Mein; . • , In conclusion, let me say that Lha •e not the.tione .t spare to dieense Trades Union matte re any /anther wit yew' correspondent: I might say al so that f;4lq, skin i not so tender es tq be easily pricked .witlietie blunt argu meets of "(Mercury" and he need no be arrald,ef again incurring "the displeasure ef yoor youthful anliespon7 dent." FRED RTOTT, 47, Orford-road, Warrington. •</p>
