      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-04-26_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>Motor Car Accident at Lyrn rn.</head>
        <p>Motor Car Accident at Lyrn rn.</p>
        <p>About two o'clock ou Monday al, ernoon a motor car belonging to Tapp' Laundry was being driven along the road near the dam at Lymm, when in conPequence of the chair' a loping, the vehicle ran into the footpath, and was overturned. In the car were riding, in addition to the driver, Mr Liveey, due of the manager, and Mr Waters,of Altrincham, an agent for Messrs Tapp, together with a boy. The occupants were all more or less severely shaken, and three of the wheels of the car were completely simashed. New w.heels were quickly brought from Manchester per motor car inlanswer to a telephonic message; the overturned car was raised, and the work of repair was, performed in an exceedingly expeditious manner. Warrington Magistrates Sworn In.</p>
        <p>In the Parade Room at the Police Station on Monday morning, seventeen bormagb mogistrates were re-sworn as administrators of the law under King Edward VII. The magistrates present were the Mayor (in the chair), Aldermen It W Francorab, Hutchinson and peaton, Councillors John Roberts and Smetburst;and Messrs John White, Xiba Armi- tage,F Monks, E J Gue,t, John Platt, John Geddes, Thotnas Birchall, J W Hayward, 8 Wright, Henry Thornton, and J R Young. There were also present Mr J Lyon Whittle, (town clerk), rind Mr (4 F White (deputy magistrates' clerk). The Mayor read the proclamation i.uthorizing the swearing-in, and than took the oath, afterwards administering it to the other gentlemen present. After taking the oath the magistrates signed the declaration.</p>
