        <p>On TORSI4I7 owning, at Winaington 011 Hall, North- with, Sir T Brunner, Bart., ML., received a demnts: tine representative of the Runcorn, WidzioS; and Northwich Di•trict T-iactiora' Associations and tto Cho hire District U.sion of Teachers, who submitted f cons &amp;ration certain important educational queetion.w Mr J F Moulton (Runcorn) introduced the deputation, which consisted of M sera R Lloyd (Chester),vi F Knott, ex-president of the Cheshire District Union (tiudensbaw) it Cookson (Ehincorn) J El•rrison, and W Epps (North- wich). Mr Lloyd addressed tbe hon. member reference to corporal punishment and the punisLin ,nt book, urginv that the honk was unnecessary, and the entry of petty offeace. mi4lat seriously affect the fui.urs cayoer of scholars. U-. sir J-ihn Brunner said he would bear very carefully in mind what bad been said, and would try to indoce the authorities to meet them upon some of the pointa (Hear, bear). Mr W F Knott introduced the subject of the formatior, of a local education authority for primary and ,v.condar, education. He urged a national system of education, with one local so' hority over a district, the authority to -in of tie whole of the educ...tion of have the oreanisati the area from the primary school to the point where the university was concerned. Sir John Brunner said he was heartily in symplthy with the aims of the rleputition this mattmand was at one with tht eposker in his d ecation of the present _ . _ . . -ird to the eliactroAt</p>
        <p>system, UP 11-ad long- fooired frwi</p>
        <p>by Parliament of a /maim) under which educitin from the lowest to !Mr highest tilhoilld be under one au`hority, for the res.son that if all claasea were elucated under the control of on- anthoritv they got rid of the div:sloi that produced so imlch scionthsbners Mr Moulton dealt with th. " CockFrton " ju,d,!tneat, and pointed to the fact that unless the judgment was immeliat.lv reversed there would be a quarter of a million adults excluded from education. Sir John sail here again he was ahroluteiy in arm pithy on this question. Tre very fact that every local authority had push,d forward the higher grades of adueation was a proof thnt the public desired it. Be zould nice them this corhf-rr, that there was a very general and atrnng feeling in the Boise of Commons that the roischi,f dine by the Ciclinrton judrement should be put yi4ot. Replying to a ftatcninnt as tC, FPCAITitV of tenure, Sir John n'surel the ~• puto lin the Crzi•if•e wa a strong feeling in favour of this in t Eco;:. f C .arn</p>
