      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-04-26_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>RUNCORN LIBRARIAN DEAD.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN LIBRARIAN DEAD.</p>
        <p>Death and Funeral of Mr. J. D. Jones. The announcement of the death of Mr. John 13 toilet Jones, Ruhcorn's well-keo vu librarian, which took places at his residence, 33, Waterloo-road, on Sunday last was received in all quarters of the town with uafeignecl regret. About 12 months awl Mr. Jones' health broke down, and he was compelled te seek a change of air in the hope of recruiting himself. He rallieci somewhat as the result of rest and careful nursing, and was able to resume his duties as librarian- It was recognirsd, how- ever, by those intimately acquainted with tarn that he was but the shadow of his former self, and his partial recovery was but a temporary matter. During the past few months he had been practically confined to the house, and despite the constant attendance of Dr McLennan, and the nursing of a devoted wife, h- gradually sank under that insidious malady known as internal cancer, and passed away peacefully on Sunday evening. Deceased rose from very humble circumstances by sheer pluck and perseverance. Toe son of the late Mr Daniel Jones, plasterer, he chose the same occupa- tion as his father, and carried on business on his own account for a number of years. On the adoption of the Free Libraiies Act in 1882, Mr Jones was appointed librarian. The library was opened by Sir James Picton in the old Board of Commissioners room near the Police Station, and it was conducted there for about twelve months. At the end of that time the movement had grown to such an extent in public favour that the ,accommodation was found to be insufficient, and the Commissioners decided to remove to the present build- ings in Waterloo-road. Eferecertain rooms were allocated to the library and simultaneously with its removal a reading-room was opened. In 1885 it was found necessary to further extend the library deportment, and an appeal Co the Commissioners resulted in more accommodation being conceded. At the close of the first year of the library's existence the total number of volumes was something like 1,500, a good proportion of these being generously given by Mr John Simpson. The premises were extended, and as the years rolled by the Library Committee found the reading-room uecomfort- ably crowded, and the need for more space made itself palpably evident. The climax was reached in 1897, when, under the will of che late Mr S B Chadwick, 2,000 volumes came as a legacy to the library. The question of extension could then be shelved no longer, and the committee, adopted a scheme prepared by Mr James Wilding, the surveil or to the District Counce, which was carried into effect at a cost of £250. The premises, as extended, were opened by Sul John T Brunner, Bert., M.P.' in October, ' 1898 In all these movements Mr Jones, as librarian, tolls a deep interest, and he spared neither time or labour to add to the popularity and usefulness of the library. Be was always most anxious te consult the convenience of borrowers, and his obligine and courteous manner earned for him great popularity among all classes of readers. At the opening of the ex- tendee premises in 1898, testimony was borne to the fact that he had filled the offise of librarian with great credit to himself, and with' entire satisfaction to the in- habitants of the town. When Mr Jones was appointed to the office, the Library Committee consisted of the Rev Canon Barclay (chairman), Dr McDougall (vice- chairman), Messrs F a Lake, J R Frita, W Hand( .y, J Meadewcroft, Dr Steele, and T Riley. Some few years ago Mr Jones took no small part in the organizing of a course of Gilchrist Lseurei, in connection with the the library which proved to be a great success: Be con- tinued in business as a plasterer until February, 1883 when he was appointed to the office of collector of rates aby the Board of Commissioners. Prior to his health breaking (town Me Jones compiled a new catalogue of the liprary books, after the most approved method, and this work stands out to-day as a monument to his interest and devotion in the offise he so worthily filled ' for a period of nineteen years. Other movements claim- ed a fair share of his attention. He was known as an enthusiastic temperance worker and for years be held the post of secretary to the Wesleyan Methodist Adult Temperance Society. Work of this kind was indeed a great hobby with him, and he was just in his element when speaking at or assisting in the organizing of a temperance meet- ing or campaign. Be was a life-long member of the Wesleyan Methodist church, and was an active mernbe• in the sebbath school and other institutiens associated with that body. His devotion to Old Brunswick was told by himself in a letter read at the Centenary Cele- bration last year. Mr Jones had taken an active part in organising the Centenary proceedings, and almost on the eve of the celebration his health gave way whits on a visit to s friend at Liverpool. The following extract from the letter referred to will not be out of order here as expressing what Mr Jones felt with regard to the place in which he revived his religious training :—" If I could bays been at the meeting on Saturday night or Mond oy night, and an opportunity had presented it- self, I should have taken great pleasure in testifying that all I have that is worth possessing, and all that I am that is worth while being, I owe, under the provi- dence of God, to Brunswick Sunday school and its great soperintendent, Mr John Simpson. I was so little when I was taken there as a scholar that I cannot recall the event. The names of four teachers stand out more prominently than the rest as influencing me—Mr Joseph Fox. Mr Charles El Kent, Mr John Ockleston Jackson, and Mr William Eaton. I joined Mr Eaton's society class in 1866, and have been a mem bee of society ever since. You know of the happy as- sociations of the Improvement Class, that wonderful monument to the faithful devotion and zeal of our dear frierel MeSinoister,a class it wou d be difficult to match in Methodism.' Mr Jones was vice-president of the im- provement class for many years. To revert to his con - nection with the library,it is worthy of note that during his regime it grew from 1,500 to something like 10,000 including, of course, the 2,000 which formed the legacy of the late M S B Chadwick.ooe of the methods which Mr Jones adopted to popularise the library was to write a series of art icles,under the title of " Half hours in the Ruacern Free Library." That the Library Committee thought these were of considerable value was shewn by the fact that they hod them reprinted and circulated among the reading public. The deceased gentlemau was nothing if he was not devoted to the town of his birth. He followed with a keen interest every move- ment for the betterment of the cfsmmunity, and he was • great admirer of Runcorn and its traditions. La politics Mr Jones ores an ardent Liberal of the old school, though be never took an active part in local affairs on account of his public offices. He Was 51 years of age, and leavee a widow her whom the deepest sympathy is felt. The Funeral. The funeral took place on Wednesday afternoon, and was largely attended. Leaving the house In Waterloo-road, the deceasea's remains, enclosed in a handsome brass-mounted oak coffin were Conveyed to St Paul's Church, High-street, where a short service was conducted by the Revs A Wells, El Morrison, and J B Foster (circuit ministers). As the body was conveyed up the aisle Mr liegioald Johns on played 0 rest in the Lord "on the organ. During the service two favourite hymns, " Jean, lover of my soul," and " Rock of ages," were sung. The Rev A Wells, in a well-chosen address,pail a high tribute to Mr Jones' public and private life, and alluded to his excellent work for and in behalf of the Church of his' adoption. Mr Jones had told him he was a Metho- dist bernialse his intellect bade him become one,but if his - judgment had told him he could serve Gee better in any other Church then he would have become • member of ' that Church. There they had the keynote of the man's life—he was broad-minded. Mr Jones bad done • great work in connection with the Church, and he,ths speak- er, was ever hearing of it, and his character stood out as an illustration they might well copy. He trusted that God would in his infinite mercy raise up someone to continue the work, and that the grace of God might be sufficient to meet the necessity of her who was seated at home mourning the loss of her dear life companion from tar side. The funeral cortege then re-assembled and moved off with melancholy step to the Greenwas -road iCemetioise, where the last rites were performed by the Rev A Wells The choir of St Paul's under Mr R Morris, sang the hymn " Peace perfect peace" at the open grave. Amonsst those who preceded the hearse were —Messrs T Sim-niter, E Taylor. P T Lawton, B Dcdd, Charles L tler, W Rushton, J Paynter, J Higgins, W Kirkham, Leadntone, 0 Millington, Thomas Reynolds, Thomas Wood, JBowyer' S Tomlinson, E fruition, J.P.. C C., S Taylor, J. P., A It N irvian J.P.. C.C., J Wilson,S Walton, M J Sweetman, U J Grine, T Riley, J F Moulton, W Asnkinsen, James Policia al, W Garrett, M Berker,James W Jones. A Austin, and J Nall, J H Weston, T A Lawrenson, William Sutton, George W Yates, Dr F McDougall, Messrs A Lancaster, T Boote, J M Frith, J R Frith, ',stewards of circuit), Joshua Millwood, and T Laws (Widnes Free Library), H &amp; Hankinson, W Shaw, Thomas F irmby (deputy librarian Liverpool Free Lit-one), J J Ogle (Bootle Library), F W Hutto's, S Pope (chairman Runcorn Free Library), B Crosby (Southport), J H Darlington, Rev S Wright, Messes w Nickson, L Scott, 8 Lhtler. T B)wyer, James Bowyer, leseeh Littler, F Beek. D Henry Jonas, J Dsuel-s. S Covsetry, T Mininger, Anderson, F I .hasen (insirie- trates' clerk, Runcorn), T Cooke (relieving officer), G Johnson. T Sanders, T Burrows, E Eedeti, T H Weate, G A Kirkham, James Lea, Richard Moore, J Ball, R Morris, J Greenhalgh, W M Cos North, and W Sirnkin. The following bearers walked each side of the hearse: —Messrs George Hayes, W,Millineton, E Kniveton, P Brown, J Green, F Hosker, P Unswortn, and George Millington. - Two coaches followed the heorse, containing the chief mourners, viz:-...Mr William Jones (uncle of the widow) and Mr Itelhard Jones (cousin of the widow), Messrs D Bishtewo, W Handley, George Blllacriy, F J Norman, ' JP J Unaworth. R Rawlinson, E Marshal!, and D, McLenl3B7l. Beautiful floral tributes of respect were received from Mr and Mrs Greenolgh ; R and E Hughes. Nottingham ; Mr and Mrs Posnett ; Brunswick Mutual Improvena. nt Diees; Brunswick Sunday School; Mr atm Mrs Unsworth and mina Percival ; Mr and Mrs R Moore ; Mr and Mrs B„!/,ma ; Mr and Mrs Rowland; and the officials of the Runcorn Urban District Council " with deepest ' sympathy." Throughout the town blinds were drawn and a heart- felt set se of mourning and sympathy with the widow . was everywhere manifezt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-04-26_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>ru. E EXAMLNER ultl,)A if, APRIL 26 1901.</head>
        <p>ru. E EXAMLNER ultl,)A if, APRIL 26 1901.</p>
        <p>BAZAAR at WARRINGTON• Splendid Effort by Bewsey-road Wesleyans. Parr Hall, W:rrington, his been the Peqlll3 during the pakt week of a grand" British Empire" Bazaar in connection with the Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church and schools. The object in view has been the raising of frnm £BOO to £l,OOO for the following purposes: (1) To provide f w the renovatir,n of the Church, (2) To repair repair and enlarge the organ, (3) ro pay off tne debt of the Courch Trost Account, (4; To help to enlarge Silver-street day</p>
        <p>11The necessity for the effJrt was fully set forth in the bazaar hand-book.'lt was:pointe I out that dur- ing the past twenty-six years the fa -ic of Bewsey- road Church has b.?come 83 dale •jorated that it hai become imperatively necessary hat a genera outside overhauling shall take place. Trio inside of the church needs cleaning and decorating. The organ is sadly in need of repair,and to to be brought up to date and rend-red as eiThient as possible should be enlarged. " The friends now worshipping at Bewsey-rond," continues the hand-book, "having to face this expenditure, an i also feeling deeply interes:.ed in Silver-street Day Scholl, have deter , mined to make an effort to raise .£l,OOO f3r these various objects by means ot this bazaar. Bewsey- road has many able and efflient worker., but although by hard work and much sell-denial these have done much,yet without the help of our friends we fear that this effort will only meet with modi- fied success. No ge ieral appeal has been made in this way for 26 years, and it is with the utmost confidence that this appeal is made. To as!hieve Ruccess is to deserve if, and those who know what has been going on for the paattwo years at Bewsey- road have no misgivings as to the merits of the case.</p>
        <p>Not only is a history of the church given, but 800:14 inters jug information is supplied of Silver- street day school, which is now being enlarged. "It may truthfully be said "says the hand book, ' that the school owes its very ex'stence to Air Guest For many years the school had immense difficulties to surmount, and many faint-hearted friends urgel the giving up of the school. Mr Guest refused to entertain the idea, and for years bore almost alone the burden of the trust. The present success of the echo )1 is largely due to his indomitable perm- verance,his successful management ot it funcis,and his unceasing advocacy of its claims. Tee school was opened ou January 20th, 1877, under the mastership of 31r Carter Spensley, with 46 children Mr Spensly remained in charge until the end of 1883. Mr 8 Barraclough corlimeuce.l work as head master in January, 1884, and completed his service in December, 1885. TM present master, Mr.) Upson, was appointed in January, 1886. The school in its earlier years had very many difficulties. The class of children was of the poor- est, the parents were utterly indifferent to the need of educating th4ir childrAn, and many only sent their children to school because the law compelled taem to do so. The local School Attendance Com- mittee had but one officer, and its attempts to enforce the law was largely unsuccessful. No favours were fec-uved from the Inwectors, and with small teaching staffs, owing to c,ippled fin inces, the results of the examinations and con- sequently the Government Grants were unsatisfac- tory. All these drawbacks have passed away. There are now 640 children on the books of the school, with a staff of 16 teachers. The reports of the Inspectors for the past few years have been highly satisfactory, and the Government grants have increased by leaps and bounds. The average attendance and the grants have been cia- tinuously prignssive for the past eight years. The school buildings have been ewarged twice, and a further enlargement is now in process, by which additional accommodation will be provided for 210 children. The school is now one of the largest and most successful in Slethodism</p>
        <p>This account of the Silver-street Day School would be incomplete iflto notice were taken of the keen interest taken in i by the late Dr Starkey Smit"). He was elected on the Board of Manage- ment soon after its commencement, and was a manager on the day of his death. His enthusiasm for its success, and his deep interest in its welfare were so marked, that after his death it was unani- mously decided to raise a fund for tne enlargement of the school as a town's memorial to him. Men of all political parties and religious opinions contribu- ted generously to the fund. The enlargement at Silver-street was carried out, and the Infanta' Sc!hool at Ssnkey Bridges was opened from the same fund. The prssent managers ate Rev T Bishop (chairman), Rev C II Hocken, Rev H G Roberts. M.A., Messrs J W Hayward, J.P., ft G Henshall, F Pendlebury, Dr R Smith, Councillor Tinnion, W Winstanlay, W Hickman, Dr J H Gornall, James D'drls, A H Dunn, R H Silcock, A Horncks, ft Dale, W Furness, and E J Guest, B.A , JP., (treasurer, secretary, and Government cor e4pondent).</p>
        <p>- , The decor ttion of the bazsar was placed in the hands of Mr G M Bridges, the talented scenic artist,and his decorations are descriptive of the ex- pansilbn of the British Empire. Under toe skilful hands of the decorator the platform has been trans- formed into a representation of the great In lian Empire. It gives a reliable idea of the luxuriance of tropical vegetation,and at the same time a vivid impression of the grandeur of oriental architecture. Through a foreground of exuberant foliage of lofty palms and graceful ferns is seen a magnificent palace., with its terraced gardens overlooking the sacred flanges. Palaces, mosques and buildings, all in brilliant and ornamental colour, con4itute a mar- vellous panorama of exceeding beauty. In the centre gallery is a magoiflcent view of the British Hous,s of Parliament, whilst along the side galleries are scenes reprrs nting the rock of Gibraltar and part of the Mitliterrane.:o Fleet ; ilong Bong, st wing the Coine4a quarter, mth Quaint buildings. dative life, &amp;c; and scenes descriptive of New Zealand, Australia and South Attica. Each stall was representative of some int,igral part oLthe British Empire,aud the goods displayo were of a character which should meet with ready purchasers. The ladies in charge bad shown great taste in setting out their wares, and visitors were given the following advice:—" If you see anything you want—by it! If you see anything you don't want—buy it for someone else." The names of the stalls and stallholders were:— , •• NEW ZYCLIAND" (Day School) : Mrs Bird, Mrs Dodds, Mrs Guest. Mrs Med.-look, Mrs tdollitt, Mrs Pugh, Mrs Upson; Misses Booth. Dodds a ,d Guest. Aurras.Lts.": Mrs Bsiie. M-s Barnshaw,Mrs Harcourt, Mrs Hocken, Mrs Stone, Mrs W Taylor: Misses B Bailey and Taylor.</p>
        <p>" SOUTH AFRICA": Mu Place, Mrs A Taylor, Urs Tianlon Mrs Winstanley and Miss Griffith..</p>
        <p>" GIBR/LTAR (iunday School): Mrs London and the Misses Hawley. Healey. Skelland and at Stone</p>
        <p>"MONO Korid" (Oho-ir): Mrs J Hannan, Mrs D S Stone, Mrs Sherlock. Mns Wadsworth; the Misses Bailey, Gough, Mason, Sinclair; and Messrs it Corbett, A Parkinson and W Parkinson.</p>
        <p>Mrs Hdelsten. Mrs Hayward, Pdrs Osborne, Mrs Povey. Mrs Robinson.' Mrs Savage ; and Miss Hayward. " WALES' (Flower Stall): Mrs Shtw Green, 6fra A Sil000k; and the Misses Show Orem, 0 Shaw Green, A Gornall act H Hayward. Os:mast:" (Sweet Stall) : The Misses G Farrell, Harvey, G Horton, Jones, A Lawton, S Moson,H Parkinson. a Pore), W Upson, Walker, Wood.</p>
        <p>.. EICATI:AND ' (Refreshment Stall): Mrs Appleton. Mrs Horton, Mrs R sallooek, Mrs Parkinson assisted by Misses Benson. Cissle Collin, Mabel Greenough, Ethel Jones, Laverock, Ethel Roberts, Madge Sileock, Weaver. The following i• a list of the officials :—President, Rev 0 Hale Hocken ; Treasure,. Mr J W Hayward ; Secret.ry, Mir J Upson : Secretary of bank. Miss Lawton (Bewsey-road) Executive Corn nittet, : Mr Appleton, Mr J adios', Mrs F Bailey, Mr R Corbett. Mr J Shaw lareen.Mrs J Shaw Green. isailded'oci%. MYS Hocken. 'Jr J Horton. . Mr F Norman. Mr W Parkin. son 1 ri .6 . Hayward. Lawton, rd Mr tt Ma son Mrs Parkinson Mr F Pendiebury, Mr H Place. Mr J Povey, Captain Savave, Mr John W Qherlock, Mrs R Silcock. Mrs Wadsworth. Mr W Win.tanley, Mrs Winstalley and the ofilmrs. Printing and Advertising Committee: Mlssrs Appleton (convener), It Corbett, I Shaw Green. J Horton, If Maddock, F Nor .._ :a. W Parkinson, F Pendlebury, .1 Povey, W Win. 1 stanley and C.ptain WA Savage. Music and Ere.ertainrnent Committee, Councillor Tinnion (chairman), Messrs 11 aough. Hannan, F Nor.nan, Oldbury, W Parkinson (convener). Pendlebury, Wadsworth, Win l' stanley, jun. ' teParsels Committee: Messrs J J Cragg (convener) Earn_ shaw. Healey, G Mitchell, A Parkinson. Sherloils, . Tai or ' (chairman). I wecirlp.:4sy afternoon. Amoriest those WEDNESDAY'S OPENING :CEREMONY. There was an OnCouraging attendance at the opening H G Roberts, M. A., John cpr:reieFent7, Yageoln.:* : —Hie TV ,r.hip tho Mayor (Alderman II Roberts), Mr Mitchell (St Helens), it •vq 1' Bishop, teren, ), c a a ~,i,,,n, yve,,leyan minirters), C Finch, (Primitive, Ellison, Brough,Peacnck, Bonnett, 'll 3G( 'I uue tpnbil'orPdilnli s l ) , IS Hughe4 (BlPtlsf). Wil i ifha (Coogreea- J.P.; John Bennett, John Tiq?,or, tionalilt), Aldermen Green and Butson, Councillors , J Upson, E NI:: tnsni irc3 n J. VirYv R therts, Hayward, Fi G. Rpnabau. ,10:eph R iiley, 4 Poesy, William Silcock, WpiaWcoi.ns jtaanuledyo,ooW. Bl'bwrkacjingswaour,ttr Appleton, Reale_ y and othcrs. The proceeding. n.muncnced %vial t,.e singing the lic mu .. All people .h it on earth do dwell." 'rho Roy C Finch ..Iter wards off..red up prayer, Tim MAYOR, who had a cordial receptyon, said the pan. wLicti had hen al:ottel to him that afternoon %tits an important one. Be w,.s glad to Wiwi an opportunity (.1 showing his practical oympathy with the objects for which that bazaar was hkitiir, htld. He had read the</p>
        <p>programme of causes for that bazaar with considerable interest. He thouelet all of them were good ones, but the one which appealed t him most was the last one,— the int Lateen to raise a fund for the enlargement of Silver-street school as a town's memorial to the late Dr r retark .37 Smith. He (the Mayor) had had the Pleasure of knowing Mr Comm ever since he came to Warring- ton and he could bear witcess to the valuable work he w.s doing in that school. He (the Mayor) had now to introduce a gentleman whose acquaintance he had made within the last ten minutes. Mr Mitchell came to them with a Rood reputation and he (the Mayor) hoped that it would be maintained in a way which would be satisfac,ory to the promoters of the Bazaar ' and le isfacroay to Mr Mitchell. Be (the Mayor) off red to him a very c vdiel and eincere welcome and ex- pressed the earneq hope that he woule give that eplen- did bezear a magnificent send-off which would ba fully evalled if not excelled on the following days. (a oplause. ) Mr. A. H. MITCHELL, of St. Helens, said he was plased to have that opportunity of coming to Warrior- ton to open the b zeal*. He had great sympathy with gentlemen who were on bazaar committers. He looked upon them as necessary evils. (Laughter.) He meant, of cnurse, the b zare, and not the gentlemen. (Laugh- ter.) He w'as sure the la ies would not consider the gentlemen ra, c •.sary evils. (H, at, hear.) Unfortun- at ly he had been engaged vet-fitly in several hazier's. H • ha i opened:one .0 Coineall,and Co Friday last he had the pleasure of °peeing another at St. Helens so that he thought by the time he had finished he weuld have had eaeugh of bazaars. (Laughter.) He was sure that from the ladies and gentlemen before him and the re- presentative platform there would be little &amp;faculty in raising the amount required. The cause for which they were met deserved every support. He would advise them to spend all they could for the more they spent, the more good would be done. Be had very great 034 sure in proenuncine the bazaar open. (Applause ) Mr J. W. HAYWARD. proposed a vote of thanks to Mr Mitchell and the Mayor for their presence. The Mayor, he said, worked indefatieably for the benefit of his fellow townsmen and it might be truly said of him that the greatest among them hal become the servant of all* (Appiause.) Mr C nancillor Tr/venni, in seconding the proposition, said the fLzaarComnittee was exceedingly fortunate in obtaining Mr Mitchell to mien the b +nate (Hear,hear.) He had giv m them some good advice as to raisivel the moley, and he bad not the least doubt Mr Mitchell w ,uld set them a good example. Referring to the Mayor Mr Tinnier' sit Alder:nen Roberts had done exneedieg- lv wdi as chief megirsate, indeed much better than some of them thought. Be had sunk every political and ,ec'arian -ling and had come forward to do all he could to further the best interest of Warrington. (Hear, , bear). The Elsar THEODORE Bisetop supported the proposition, and rererred i eu 0 ,i‘ric terms to the serviees of Mr Mil chell and th Mayor. Tee vote of thanks was eine') imounly carried. Mr Mercume, and • the MAYOR ackeowledged the corn- plimew, and business was I.“n ceeimenced.</p>
