      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-04-26_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>IMPORTANT EARLESTOWN APPEAL CASE.</head>
        <p>IMPORTANT EARLESTOWN APPEAL CASE.</p>
        <p>The Police and Stolen Property.</p>
        <p>- . . A, me of /pale importance to the police in general and the county police at E.rlestown in particular is occuryin,t,' the attention of the Lord Chief Justice and Mr Justice Lawrance, in the King's Bench Division of the High Court. just at prrsent. It is that of Winter v. Bank, alti another, which is the appeal rf Polie.- serges..cs Banks and Lings, of the Lancashire county police, stationed at Earlestown, from a decision of Mr Gwynne James, a county court judge at Warrington, in favour of toe plaintiff, who sued for damages in respect of a gig which he alleged had been stolen from him, and which the defendant,on the instructien of their sup-rior officer,ha I given up to the alleged ttnef.John Broderick, who had been tried at the Liverpool Quarter Sessions and acquitted. The &amp;reactants ask that the j idgment of the county court judge bo set aside and judgment entered for the defendants on the grounds that (1) there was no evidence to support the finding of the county court judge ; t 2) that by the sale of the gig to lehn Broderick in market overt Broderick acquired a good title, and the property had never 13,-en r-invested in tip plaintiff ; (3) that the gig had come into posse,- silo of the ponce in connection with a criminal charze preferred against Broderick, and be having been acquit- trd, the right to the poss. sion of the property was in Broderick and nor the police ; and (4) the possession of the property WAS giv n up to Broderick by order of the deiced ent,' superior officer. Mr Sutton, who represented the appellants, argued that the del oidants are not liable. , and if anybo iy euzlit to he fuel it ought to be the Chief I Constable of Lancashire, and that under the circum- stances tie police are 0 t liable, as they were entitled to deliver up tha property to the person from whom it had been taken when the charge of theft was nreferred. Mr Trevor Lloyd,who ay:mired for the phinciff,zaid gilt as a matter a' fact the gig had been stolen duriog tho half- hour that the plaintiff had been spending at an inn taking refremhments. ea had not soki it in m•rket overt or parted willingly with ita possession. the Lord (Thief Justice said that in view of the import ance of the case the court would take time to consider its decision.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-04-26_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>FRODSHAM•</head>
        <p>FRODSHAM•</p>
        <p>A HORSE'S TERRIBLE FALL —Oa IMondav last valu- able horse b goosing to Mr 1-1 Linaksr. Church-street, Frodsham, met sato a terlble death. It appeared that the horse vas engaged in preparing a (lel I at OvertOn for potatoes, whoa suddenly it took fright ran down the field and over the ,•mharkment into the road fslling a distarce of about 30 ce•-•t. It was instantly kilted, and 13- cat t which wal behind it was smashed to atoms. A P.,ung coachman nitned B'onsdale had a narrow escape.</p>
