      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-04-26_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS. TO-DAY—F-I,CIAL TIE. Warrington's Prospects.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS. TO-DAY—F-I,CIAL TIE. Warrington's Prospects.</p>
        <p>[By " Ovalist.'')</p>
        <p>It is fifteen years since Warrington were in the final of a Cup Coropmtition, and on tne last occasion —the season of 1885,6- -they were successful and secured the Smth-West Lancashire and Border Powns trophy. Those were the days when the" wire- , pullers " were in their prime, and were known far 1 and near as one of the best clubs in the County Palatine. Since then the Warrington Club has always held its own in cluo matches and Competi- tion football, but has never achieved great things' " though coming very near it in 1896-7 when the semi-final of the Northern Union Cup C mipetition was reaclid, and the Batley Club ex.ingui.hed Warrington's hopes, That result everybody in War- rington is hoping to see reversed to-day, for War- rington and Batley meet once more, and this time they have to fight for the cup itself in the final tie at ileadingley, Leeds. Warrington have not only distinguish -d themselves this season by fighting their way to the concluding stage of the premier Rugby Competition in the North, but they have per- formed with a similar brilliance and success in the South-West Lancs. and Border Towns Cup Competi- , don, which they win once more if they get the better of Leigh at Wigan on Monday evening.</p>
        <p>This is therefore,a great year in the annals of the Warrington Football Club,and the fifteen now doing duty so gallantly have the opportunity of eclipsing all previous records, and placing Warrington's name high up on the scroll of fame. Even the"old stagers " will be silenced if the present team lands both cups—and I, for one, am confident that they will do so. Warrington has been so long in the rut of mediocrity that it seems only fitting they should rise suddenly and astound evsrybody with their performanc -8. And there is no questioning the fact that ihey have astonished everybo ly during the last few weeks. Ever BiCeo the fir, t round of the CLIP Competition at Leeds,when the Warrington men surpassed themselves, they have trodden the path of victory with no uncertain stop, Efeckmond- wike, Leeds Parish Church, Bradford and Ca-tleford —all Yorkshire clubs—having been vanquished with ease, the Bradfordians excepted, It is one of the strange features of Warrington's Northern Union Cup-tie career this seasor, that they have not met a single Lancashire club, and now, in the final, they still have Yorkshire foemen to face. Needless to say Warrington are all the better pleased, and the men are very anxious to complete the rout of the Tykes by upsetting the pretensions of the gallant youths" of Batley, and at the same time pay off old scores. There seems this selson particularly, to be a marked disparity between Lan- cashire and Yorkshire football, and in almost every instance when clubs from the opposing shires have met the represontatives of the Red Rose have been triumphant. The Yorkshiremen appear to have neglected the development of back-play, and it is in this department of the game that Cie Lancas- trians have had the pull. Wi-h the exception of a solitary try, the whole of the 82pins Warringtrin ' have scored in the Northern Union Cup-ties have come from members of the back division, who have in every match proved much superior to their oppo- nents, and it is this extra cl-verness behind that Warrington rely upon to peat Batley. They have improved tremendously .in scoring power, and a comparison of the cup-tie records of to-day's con- testants for the cup is all in favour of Warrington The latter's try-getting ability has been as marked as Batley's has been deficient, while the defence of the two team l appears to be on a par. Toe com- parative records are as follow : Warrington. For. Against. G. T. P. G. T. P. v Leeds v Ffeckmonciwike v Leeds Parish Ch I ... 3 ... II 0 ... 0 ... 0 v Bradford 2 . 2 .. 10 I ... 2 ... 8 v Castiefor</p>
        <p>1111..20 ...82 3 ... 3 ...15 Batley. V Wand A bye V Huddersfield V St Helene 'V Runcorn 1 1 5 1 ... 0 ... a V Oldham 3</p>
        <p>9 ... 3 ... 27 4 ... 1 ...</p>
        <p>It will thus be seen that Warrington have com- piled 'B2 points against clubs quite as strong as those Batley have only been able to record 27 points against. There is surely good ground here for predicting the success of Warrington. Ba:loy's biggest advantage lies in that quality krown as " old-headednes," and their experience as cup- fighters. There is no discounting such a fact as three appearances in the final tie in five years and two successes—which is Batley's honourable record. A performance of this class entitles them to great rsspsct, and that respect they will get from Wai- -1 rington. The wearers of "primrose and blue" are I not going to Leeds with the anticioation that they have only to appear there to eqniai their achieve- ments of the previous visit when the Leeds team were the victim.. Bacley's tactical skill and fine , defence at once dispel any such idea, but seeing that our own defence has stood the test so well, we may be pardoned for feeling confident that the attack will win the thy. Then there is the youth- fulness and ow-rgy of the Warrington team to be counted as an asset. Of the fifteen who met Batley I in the semi-final four years ago only three—not two as I ttated recently—are i-till in -the Warring- ton ranks. These three are Isherwood (captain), Scholtxe and Swift. On the-other hand Batley have nine of their original team, viz; Garner, Davies, Fi zzerald, Goo iall. Oakland, Stubley. Spurr, Maine and Fisher. In point of years therefore the York- shire team are at some disadvantage. and I hope the axiom that" youth will be served" will once again be proven. The published weights of the two teams show that they are well-matched, War rington—for a wonder -possessing a slight advan- tage. Details:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-04-26_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>Warrington.</head>
        <p>Warrington.</p>
        <p>Age. Height. Weight. J Hallam full be J Flsh three qua, , r back... 21 D I.herwood (eapt) ~ ... 24 G Dickenson ~ ... 19 B Harris .• 20 11 Bate half back J Duckworth. A 9 Blardman forward J Fell .. WHdosnisdion .. ........ 25 A Soholtze . J Eden , - 1.!I 12 6 J Ounnir -- -</p>
        <p>_ _.inninghnrn .. .... 22 11 Morrison , 7 Swift . ,</p>
        <p>ball Wit 9 until quite recently. The two railway companies are vieing with each other in their effort to accommodate the crowd and about four special trains are le tying each of the two stations (Arpley and Centrsi). Some sixty saloons have be,n c•!ar- tared to convey special pattie, and there would have been more hat not the supply run out.</p>
        <p>By the way, one lively crew intends to startle the L ;ads folk. I hear that about twenty fellows are going in tall hate, wit' i primrose and blue bands and streamers. In additi +EI they are to carry a fishing roi eac4 with a "fish" in c3ub colours at the end. The one and only Fish needs but to dis- tinguish himself by playing at his best to-day, to be airno-t immortalised in the tuturo.</p>
        <p>A Batley journalist who is almost as fa-nilar with the play of the principal clubs in Laneshire as with that of the Yorkshire teaps, writes The "Gallant Youths" wilr have no advantage in weight over the Warringtonians, and there will be little to choose between the two packs. Batley may claim some little advantage in the scrimmages but Warrington will heat them in following up. especially if this hot weather continues. The War- ringtonians will be weak.st, in comparison with Batley, at half back.</p>
        <p>Both Oakland and Midg'ey are smart tacklers, and Oakland in particular is a very crafty player. His moves are not always above criticism, as he is sometimes too fond of punting over the sciimmage when a pass woold serve his ei te better, but all in all the Batley skipper is a very tricky little fellow Near the line he is as dangerous a player as can be found in the Union, his try against Olonam a fortnight ago proving that assertion up to the hilt. In Batley. Oakland is the cup-tie hero—ne ba snatched so many games out of the fire.</p>
        <p>• Warrington's chances are considerably improved by the acnident woich places Isherwood and Fish against Fozgeral I and Auty. If Goldall and Davies had besn on tha side of the field Batley folk would never have doubted the issue for a moment, as Goodall' s defence is equal to anything, and Davies is probaWy one of the best defensive players that has ever been drawn from Wales. Fitzgerald is no the most reliable of centres, but he is an experi- enced and cool player, and cony spoil Isherwood's passes. Auty is Batley's youtigest three-quarter, and though Fish is no older '' Jack "is a much more polished player.</p>
        <p>Batley's hops is that Goodall and Davies,who ar both strong on tie wrack, may find the Lancashire backs something to do besides feeding the left wing, and thus automatically relieve the pre.sure on Fitzgerald and Auty,though there is a quiet con- fidence that the defence of even those two will be equal to the strain.</p>
        <p>It must not be forgotten that the best teams in the Union have found it impossible to set their passing machinery into motion agsinst the 'gallant youths," because if the opposing forwards get possession the Batley scrimmagers extend them- selves for cackling purposes. Batley's defence is certainly a word to conjure witti,and if Warrington break it down they will achieve a triumph tb at wit entitle i hem to the honour, of the day But all Yorkshire doubts their ability to succeed where Oldham, Runcorn, and St flelens have failed.</p>
        <p>I may add (if it is 1,;--o-r-t-h-rrhile) that there is a great d-al of betting—much mord than one cares to see—and odds of 6 and 7 to 4 are being laid on Batley.</p>
        <p>Batley p'ayers are training only in the ordinary way, two nights a week on the ground. The com- mittee have always insisted on the player i being kept at work during the cup-ties ; that has proved the best mode This was done before the Profes- sional Laws (Employment Gduie) were passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-04-26_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>Batley.</head>
        <p>Batley.</p>
        <p>Age. Height. Weight. Garner, full back ........... ...... 29 Cartes. three-quarter blok ... 23 Fitzgerald .. 27 Goodall, Auty „ ... 19 Oakland, half back Midgley , Fisher. forward Rodger , 1 Stubley , Spurr „ Maine „ . Fortml „ • Carroll . Hollingwortk</p>
        <p>_ Since the easy victory over Castleford in the semi-final at Broughton a fortnight ago, Warring- ton have been ratting and training quietly. Tee men .have, of course. remaine 1 at their work, and the only training considered nec,ssary has been walking, running, and punting in the evenings, followed by systematic rubbing-down. Fell has quite got over the injury to his ankle, and Board- man is pronounced free from the cold which troubled him last week. Bate's leg is also said to be right again. The men are all taking great care of thens;lves, and it is expected that they will enter the Hendiogley enclosure " fit" enough to fight for a kingdom.</p>
        <p>If county partizanship is not too strong, Warring- ton ought to get a go xl reception at L4eds' seeing that they vanquished two of the city clubs in the earlier stages of the competition. Still, as this is a " war of the roses," too much backing must not Ife expected from the Yorkshire crowd. Pro- bably it will not be needed, for, given fine weather on this riruientous occasion, quite 5,000 Warrington folk are expected to follow the team. The excitement and enthusiasm locally are prodig- ious, and you meet every day people who are going to Leeds who have never known what Rugby foot- -1</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-04-26_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>West Lancashire Cup.</head>
        <p>The West Lancashire Cup.</p>
        <p>Thsup,h the interes in the struggle for the West Lancashire and Border Towns Cup is by no means so keen as that for the Northern Union Cup still, for old associatioirt sake, plenty of interest is taken in it in Warringtori- As above indicated, Warrington woo this cup in the year it was first c)mpated for, viz , 1886, the contestant.; in the final tie being Warrington and " Tommy" Barnes winning the match for Warrington with one of his seneational dropped goals. After a few years the competition •x- -, pired, and was only revived last season. when St Helens won it. They had an easy task, however, as neither Swinton nor Warrington completed their fixturea,owing Co disagreements which were not to the credit of either club. This season the competition has been better man- aged, and Warrington have reached the final by beating Widnes and St Helens. Leigh are the other finalists, and, barrirg a draw at Leeds to-day, the final will trt played at Wigan on Monday evening, kick-off at 6 30.</p>
        <p>Warrington fully expect to secure the trophy, and judging by the previous meetings between Warrinr ton and Loidh this seasrxi, they s'iould not have much diffi- culty in doing so. The principal event is, of course, the one on Saturday. but Warrington are sery anxious to bring both cups home, and I hope toey will.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-04-26_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>Th,_e Association Cup.</head>
        <p>Th,_e Association Cup.</p>
        <p>I must congratulate the Warrington eleven on their success in winning the Warrington nd District Cop last Saturday. Tbey have performed creditably through. out the season and won many friends among the I admirers of the sister code. Warrington had Newton as opponents and playing before an Esrlertown crowd they woo by three goals to one,R Pierpoint notching two of the points. Rugby followers consider their Association friends' successA good omen, and there is plenty of attention paid to omens in football matters.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-04-26_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>Cricket.</head>
        <p>Cricket.</p>
        <p>Before football has made its exit,cri ckot—the premier summer sport—is once again with us, and ideal weather favoured the opening of the season at Warrington nil Saturday* As vet, whilst in the throes of fmtball cup finals, Warringtonians do not app.ar to be taking much interest in toe game,and the attendance at Arpley on Saturday was extiemely m.agre. As has been the css for some years oast. the firs ,1 nrcrure of the s-•sein was with Salton. The Warrington eleven was consti- tuted similar to !at year's, with the exe-ption that M, Gaorge Clarke was unable to turn out,owing to a serious indisposition which will probably prevent him from tak- ing any active part in cricket this season.</p>
        <p>The ground wiginspleadid condition, and the local wielders of the willow wrra fortunate in winning the toss and taking first turn at the wicket. For about a quarter of an hour matters looked very unpromising for the home side. 2iif Thewlis, the professional. waa dis- missed with a " duck " by Bsswell, while B Silcock and J Callon retired with only 13 runs on the board.</p>
        <p>Matters brightened up however,when Dr Smith and F C Johnson took the field, but the latter was bowled by Soulsby after contributing 17 runs in fine style. Thei score uow stood at 33 for four wickets. Johnson's place was filled W Pocimore, who was, however at once dismissed by Boswell.</p>
        <p>W Bibby, the Warrington Captain, was in capical I form, and in company with Dr Smith, he made a solid stand. 139 was at length bowled by Boswell, having knocked up 23,,without a faulty stroke. Be was heartily applauded at the close of his innings.</p>
        <p>Dr Smith still exhibited a sound defence, and shortly after being joined by W. M. Silnca, completed his fifty, and raised the total score to the century. W M Silcock maintained his reputation as a " miter.' and hit out freely. Me ball was driven in all directions and the batsman bad contributed eleven when he-was run out. Both J Briggs and G Elampqon were bowled by Bowen, without obtalning a single run. At 134, Warrington declared their innings closed.</p>
        <p>The feature of the innings was Dr Smith's brilliant battings that player e.rrying out his bat after rattling up 64. He made no less than five boundary strokes, and the skilful manner in which he dPaYs with the balls sent down, was highly nm,lented upon.</p>
        <p>Like Warrington, Sefton commenced their innings it. anything but brilliant style. Both BlAke and Owen were bowled by the home protessional alter scoring a solitars,. run each. Clegg and F Farrington made matters livelier and they practically saved the match. Cleeg batted with confidence and made an invaluable 28 whilst Par- rington knocked un 9 runs, At 6,30 the Sefton aggre- gate had reached 58 for the loss of six wickets, the re- sult of the match therefore being a draw much in favour of Warrington.</p>
        <p>The second eleven fared badly at Sefton Park. Batt- ing first the visitors were dismissed after cot/411110g the small total Pf 64 runs. F Beckett working great havoc with the ball. Only B H Evans and E E Woods reached double figures. The Seftonians were in good form and had had knocked up 99 when the last wicket fell. T L Williams was instrumental in au zmenting the score by 34, and for the home team Schofield gave a fine dis- play of bowling, taking seven wickets for 21 runs.</p>
        <p>To NMTsEne.—Blin. Winslow's Soothing Syrup has been sed for over fifty years by millions of mothers for their 3hlldren while teething, with perfect success. It will relieve the roorsufferer Immediately It Is pleasant to taste ; and produces natural noiet steep, oy relieving the child from pain Hid the Hut cherub awakes" as b-Ight,as a button," Or all Chemists It lid per bottle. Advt.</p>
      </div>
