it re minder them that that power, which the tower was 411 outward symbol, lay witfl the reach of all. Brit there were four great condi, which they as God's servants must fu'fil if they were V? receive the power fr In on high- First and toreros.' was faith in God. The early Christians were remark able for their faith. The Holy Spirit was the real perel to whom they look id and upon whom they rested ; there was the secret of their heroic lives and th' peaceful deaths, of their prevailing words and tis trustful brethren. To day they honoured the Holy ptl indeed. They praised Him in every Gloria they Sol and i xpreseed their belief in Him every time they peated the Creed, commemorated Him every Whitsi tide, and some of the collects in the noble-t terms of langua.4e were addressed to Him. It they really blipi then, they were temples of God the Holy Gill Ought they to be content to lead the worldy awl eartl lives that many were leading ? Did they believe in ' great promise : "It ye walk in the spirit ye shall fulfil the lusts of the fish "? Yet they so often aye!, the wrreing to fight the battle against sin. Were t °lntent to let cruelty, impiety and infidelity wb they saw around them to c intinue without es eking to more than they were to check and remove them ? 14% they had not the spiritual power as their tore fathers 10 the Church of Christ bad it. the second conditirn el 4 submission. It was said that the separate will Ili, caused half the trouble in the world It bad certair': caused half the weakness in the church. If only tle Were to place themselves in the bands of God te used by. Him as He thought at, and not be as Arettro bishop Ferielow's exeression " We don't want Goci,,B will but what we wish," so that in doing Gud's had our own way. The third condition was love, cf could GOI bless his church as a whole when spirit and division and discord were so rife among tbe;e, It was like a scorching wind which withered fairest fhwers of the garden, the bitter :fir 'roa